1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Basiliscus
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign	9 January 475 – August 476
2 CONT
2 CONT Full name	Flavius Basiliscus
2 CONT
2 CONT Died	winter 476–477
2 CONT
2 CONT Place of death	Cappadocia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor	Zeno, deposed 
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor	Zeno, restored 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife	Aelia Zenonis 
2 CONT
2 CONT Offspring	Marcus, Caesar and later joint Augustus 
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal House	House of Leo 
2 CONT
2 CONT Flavius Basiliscus[1] (d. 476/477) was an Eastern Roman Emperor of the House of Leo, who ruled briefly (9 January 475–August 476), when Emperor Zeno had been forced out of Constantinople by a revolt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Basiliscus was the brother of Empress Aelia Verina, the wife of Emperor Leo I (457–474). His relationship with the emperor allowed him to pursue a military career that, after minor initial successes, ended in 468, when he led the 
2 CONC disastrous Byzantine invasion of Vandal Africa, in one of the largest military operations of Late Antiquity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins and early career
2 CONT
2 CONT Likely of Balkan origin,[2] Basiliscus was the brother of Aelia Verina, wife of Leo I. It has been argued that Basiliscus was uncle to the chieftain of the Heruli, Odoacer. This link is based on the interpretation of a fragment by
2 CONC  John of Antioch (209.1), which states that Odoacer and Armatus, Basiliscus' nephew, were brothers.[3] However, not all scholars accept this interpretation, since sources do not say anything about the foreign origin of Basiliscus.
2 CONC [4] It is known that Basiliscus had a wife, Zenonis, and at least one son, Marcus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Basiliscus' military career ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Fall and death
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT In August 476, Zeno besieged Constantinople.[24] The Senate opened the gates of the city to the Isaurian, allowing the deposed emperor to resume the throne. Basiliscus fled to sanctuary in a church, but he was betrayed by Acacius 
2 CONC and surrendered himself and his family after extracting a solemn promise from Zeno not to shed their blood. Basiliscus, his wife Aelia Zenonis and his son Marcus were sent to a fortress in Cappadocia,[25] where Zeno had them enclo
2 CONC sed in a dry cistern, to die from exposure.[2][26]
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basiliscus
2 CONT
2 CONT In hungarian:
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Augusztus végén Zénó harc nélkül vonult be Konstantinápolyba. Basiliscus és családja egy templomban keresett menedéket, és csak akkor voltak hajlandóak előjönni, amikor Zénó megígérte, hogy nem ontja vérüket. Ez így is történt: a 
2 CONC bitorlót, feleségét és fiát a kappadókiai Limnae-be száműzték, és halálra éheztették.
2 CONT
