1 NOTE {geni:about_me} >>>King Theoderich & his second wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>b)         ['''https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000002307392273?from_flash=1&through=6000000002307392273&fsession_id=1532246545445 THEODECHILDIS] [Techilde]? ([516/20]-[570/595]).  Procopius records that "Varnis…Hermegis
2 CONC clus” married "Theodeberti Francorum regis sororem" after the death of his first wife, that she gave birth to "Radiger", for whom his father requested marriage with "puellæ…natione Brittiæ, cuius frater tunc temporis Rex Anglorum 
2 CONC erat", and that Radiger later married his own stepmother after his father died[93].  It is possible that the name of this daughter was Theodechildis but, as will be explained, the link is tentative.  Three different primary source
2 CONC s name a Queen Theodechildis.  Fortunatus, dated to the late 6th century, wrote an epitaph to “Theodechildis Reginæ”, commenting that "frater, genitor, conjux, avus, atque priores" of his eponymous subject were "regius ordo"[94]. 
2 CONC  Gregory of Tours, in one of his lesser-known works, records the return of "Nunninus…tribunus" from Auvergne and his entry in Auxerre "tempore…Theudechildæ reginæ" after giving tribute which he had collected "de Francia" to the sa
2 CONC me queen[95].  Flodoard, in his mid-9th century history of the church of Reims, names "Teudechildi, prefatæ reginæ [=Suavegotta regina] filiæ" when recording that her mother allowed her daughter the usufruct of property which she 
2 CONC donated to the church of Reims, and later that Theodechildis made her own testamentary donation of the same property to Reims[96].  It is probable that these three sources all refer to the same person:  the only reference to anoth
2 CONC er person named "Theodechildis" in the mid- to late-6th century relates to the concubine of King Charibert, who was a shepherd’s daughter (see below).  However, none of the sources specifies that Queen Theodechildis was the daught
2 CONC er of King Theoderich.  In addition, there is no source which confirms that "Suavegotha" was the name of Theoderich’s wife, as discussed more fully above.  There are three indications that Queen Theodechildis may have been King Th
2 CONC eoderich’s daughter, and if so that she may also have been the same daughter who married the two kings of the Warnes.  Firstly, Fortunatus and Gregory accord the title "regina" to her, which provides the possible link to the Proco
2 CONC pius text concerning the king’s daughter.  This is particularly relevant as so few individuals were described in contemporary sources as "queen" and therefore the number of alternative possible co-identities is restricted.  Second
2 CONC ly, the common use of the root "Theode-" in the first part of the two names suggests a close relationship.  Thirdly, the chronology is favourable.  Little help is provided by the history written by Gregory of Tours, the most thoro
2 CONC ugh contemporary source for early Merovingian history, which does not refer to any daughter of King Theoderich I.  The reference to Auxerre in Gregory’s other work suggests a connection with Burgundy, which was ruled by King Gontr
2 CONC an at the time (see below).  Assuming that the co-identity between Theodechildis and the daughter of King Theoderich is correct, it is possible that she retired to Burgundy after being repudiated by her second husband.  One remain
2 CONC ing possible thread to trace further is indicated by Settipani, who states that her nephew King Theodebald succeeded as king in 547 "sous la régence de sa tante Theodechildis"[97].  However, the author does not provide the source 
2 CONC reference on which he bases this statement.  There is no way therefore at present to verify whether the primary source in question includes the crucial link between the phrase "sa tante" and the name "Theodechildis".  [A charter d
2 CONC ated 2 Oct [499], classified as spurious in the collection, of "Clodoveus rex Francorum" purports to be written when "filia mea…Theodechildis" was becoming a nun[98].  The editor of the Monumenta Germaniæ Scriptores series assumes
2 CONC  that this charter refers to the daughter of King Theoderich[99], but if this is correct the document must be misdated.  Another charter, also classified as spurious, in the name of "Theodechildis filia Chlodoveo" purports to reco
2 CONC rd a donation to the monastery of St Peter at Sens dated Sep 569[100].]  m firstly ([540]) as his [second] wife, [https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000005747728252?from_flash=1&through=6000000002307392273&fsession_id=15322465
2 CONC 45445 HERMENGISEL] King of the Warnes, son of --- (-before 547).  m secondly (before 547, repudiated [547/50]) her stepson, [https://www.geni.com/people/Radegis-dos-Warnes/6000000005747728262?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1532246545445
2 CONC &through=6000000002307392273 RADEGIS] of the Warnes, son of HERMENGISEL King of the Warnes & his [first] wife "http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#ClovisIB MEDLANDS'''
