1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theudebald
2 CONT
2 CONT Theudebald or Theodebald (in modern English, Theobald; in French, Thibaud or Théodebald; in German, Theudowald) (c. 535–555), son of Theudebert I and Deuteria, was the king of Metz, Rheims, or Austrasia—as it's variously called—fr
2 CONC om 547 or 548 to 555.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was only thirteen years of age when he succeeded and of ill health. However, the loyalty of the nobility to his father's memory preserved the peace during his minority. He married Waldrada, daughter of the Lombard king Wacho an
2 CONC d his stepsister (a sister of his father's second wife). This marriage fortified the alliance betweent Austrasia and Lombardy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, Theudebald could not hold on to the conquests of his father in the north of Italia. The Byzantine Emperor Justinian I sent an army under the command of Narses in 552 and, like his father before him, Theudebald avoide
2 CONC d direct confrontation with it.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a prolonged sickness and prostration, he died in 555. His realm passed finally outside of the family of Theuderic I and was united to the kingdoms of his great-uncle Clotaire I, who would soon become king of all the Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/590579/Theodebald
2 CONT
2 CONT also spelled Theudebald, French Théodebald or Thibaud, German Theudowald
2 CONT
2 CONT died 555
2 CONT
2 CONT Merovingian king of Reims from 547, in succession to his father, Theodebert I. He proved incapable of continuing the latter’s dynamic policies, especially in Italy. He left no son, and on his death his kingdom passed to his grandu
2 CONC ncle, Chlotar I.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Theudebald or Theodebald (in modern English, Theobald; in French, Thibaud or Théodebald; in German, Theudowald) (c. 535–555), son of Theudebert I and Deuteria, was the king of Metz, Rheims, or Austrasia—as it's variously called—fr
2 CONC om 547 or 548 to 555.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was only thirteen years of age when he succeeded and of ill health. However, the loyalty of the nobility to his father's memory preserved the peace during his minority. He married Waldrada, daughter of the Lombard king Wacho an
2 CONC d his stepsister (a sister of his father's second wife). This marriage fortified the alliance betweent Austrasia and Lombardy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, Theudebald could not hold on to the conquests of his father in the north of Italia. The Byzantine Emperor Justinian I sent an army under the command of Narses in 552 and, like his father before him, Theudebald avoide
2 CONC d direct confrontation with it.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a prolonged sickness and prostration, he died in 555. His realm passed finally outside of the family of Theuderic I and was united to the kingdoms of his great-uncle Clotaire I, who would soon become king of all the Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>a)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Theudebert-I-king-of-the-Franks-at-Reims/6000000004191587113  THEODEBERT] ([499/504]-end 547).  Gregory of Tours names Theodebert as son of Theoderich, specifying that he was born before
2 CONC  the death of his paternal grandfather[67].  His birth date range is narrowed more precisely to [499/504] on the assumption that he was a young adolescent when he led the Frankish campaign against the Danes, dated to 515: Gregory 
2 CONC of Tours records that his father sent him "with a powerful army" to repel the Danish invasion led by Chlochilaich[68].  The Liber Historiæ Francorum records that "Theudericus et Theudobertus filius eius et Chlotharius rex" invaded
2 CONC  Thuringia and attacked "Ermenfredum regem Toringorum"[69].  He succeeded his father in 533 as THEODEBERT I King of the Franks at Reims, Gregory of Tours recording that his childless uncle Childebert then adopted him as his heir[7
2 CONC 0].  Gregory records King Theodebert's campaign in northern Italy, which he appears to date to before the death of Queen Wisigardis which is recorded in the following section[71].  Theodebert subjugated Pannonia and threatened to 
2 CONC attack Byzantium across the Danube.  He was killed while hunting[72].  Gregory of Tours records that he died in the fourteenth year of his reign, and 37 years after the death of his paternal grandfather[73].  The Marii Episcopi Av
2 CONC enticensis Chronica records the death in 548 of "Theudebertus rex magnus Francorum"[74].  m firstly ([end 533/early 534]) as her second husband, [https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000005747728200?through=6000000080474519963&f
2 CONC rom_flash=1&fsession_id=1532243868730 DEOTERIA], widow of -, daughter of -.  Gregory of Tours records that Theodebert seduced Deoteria, wife of an inhabitant of Cabrières near Béziers, after his betrothal to Wisigardis, and in a l
2 CONC ater passage that he married her after the death of his father[75].  According to Gregory of Tours, Theodebert deserted her after being pressured to marry his previous betrothed, but refused to take her back after his second wife 
2 CONC died[76].  m secondly (betrothed before 533, 540) [https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000000320798442?through=6000000080474519963&from_flash=1&fsession_id=1532243868730 WISIGARDIS], daughter of [https://www.geni.com/profile/in
2 CONC dex/6000000002152926811?through=6000000000320798442&from_flash=1&fsession_id=1532243868730 WACHO] King of the Lombards & his second wife [https://www.geni.com/people/Austrigusa-of-the-Gepids/6000000001625377656?from_flash=1&fsessi
2 CONC on_id=1532243868730&through=6000000002152926811 Ostrogotha] of the Gepides (-[541/42]).  Paulus Diaconus names "Wisigarda…[et] secunda Walderada" as the two daughters of King Wacho & his second wife, specifying that Wisigarda marr
2 CONC ied "Theodeperto regi Francorum[77].  Gregory of Tours records that Theoderich betrothed his son Theodebert to "Wisigard, a king's daughter" and in a later passage that Theodebert married her "seven years [after he] had become eng
2 CONC aged to [her]" after being pressured to desert Deoteria but that Wisigardis "soon died"[78].  m thirdly ([542/47]) -.  Gregory of Tours records that Theodebert "married another woman" after his second wife died but gives no detail
2 CONC s[79].  [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#ClovisIB MEDLANDS] 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Theodebert & his first wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>i)          [https://www.geni.com/people/de-Austrasie-e-Auvergne/6000000005747728205?through=6000000005747728200 daughter]([532/33] or before-drowned Verdun -).  Gregory of Tours records that Deoteria bore a daughter to Theod
2 CONC ebert, who left mother and child at Clermont-Ferrand when he returned to assert his claim to the throne on learning that his father was dying[80].  In a later passage, he records that this daughter drowned in the river after her m
2 CONC other tipped her over a bridge in Verdun "afraid that the king might desire her and take advantage of her"[81]. [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#ClovisIB MEDLANDS]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>ii)         '''[https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000002512608885?through=6000000002512608885&from_flash=1&fsession_id=1532245262232 THEODEBALD] ([534]-555).  Gregory of Tours names Theodebald as the son of Theodebert an
2 CONC d his wife Deoteria, implying that he was born after his parents' marriage[82].  He succeeded his father in 547 as THEODEBALD I King of the Franks at Reims, "sous la régence de sa tante Theodechildis"[83].  Settipani does not prov
2 CONC ide the source reference on which he bases this last statement.  On Theodebald’s death, his territory was taken by his great uncle King Clotaire.  Gregory of Tours records that he had a stroke and could not move from the waist dow
2 CONC n, dying in the seventh year of his reign[84].  The Marii Episcopi Aventicensis Chronica records the death in 555 of "Theudebaldus rex Francorum"[85].  m ([554]) as her first husband, WALDRADA, daughter of WACHO King of the Lombar
2 CONC ds & his second wife Ostrogotha of the Gepides.  The Origo Gentis Langobardorum names "Wisigarda…secundæ Walderada" as the two daughters of Wacho & his second wife, specifying that Waldrada married "Scusuald regis Francorum" and l
2 CONC ater "Garipald"[86].  The Historia Langobardorum names "Waldrada" as Wacho's second daughter by his second wife, specifying that she married "Chusubald rex Francorum"[87].  Paulus Diaconus names "Wisigarda…[et] secunda Walderada" 
2 CONC as the two daughters of King Wacho and his second wife, specifying that Waldrada married "Cusupald alio regi Francorum" and later "Garipald"[88].  Gregory of Tours names "Vuldetrada" as the wife of King Theodebald[89].  Herimannus
2 CONC  names "Wanderadam" wife of "Theodpaldus rex Francorum" when recording her second marriage to "Lotharius rex patris eius Theodeberti patruus"[90].  According to Gregory of Tours, King Clotaire "began to have intercourse" with the 
2 CONC widow of King Theodebald before "the bishops complained and he handed her over to Garivald Duke of Bavaria"[91], which does not imply that Clotaire married Waldrada.  She [married secondly], her first husband's great-uncle, Clotai
2 CONC re I King of the Franks, and thirdly (after 555) Garibald Duke in Bavaria. [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#ClovisIB MEDLANDS]'''
2 CONT
