1 NOTE {geni:about_me} CHILPERICH, son of CLOTAIRE I [Chlothachar] King of the Franks 
2 CONT * m firstly ([549]) AUDOVERE, daughter of --- (-murdered 580).   
2 CONT * m secondly (564) GALSWINTHA of the Visigoths 
2 CONT * '''m thirdly (568) FREDEGONDE, daughter of --- (-597, bur Paris, Saint-Germain-des-Prés).  Gregory of Tours records that King Chilperich married Frédégonde before his marriage to Galswintha, specifying that a great quarrel ensue
2 CONC d between the two of them[324].  It is assumed that this indicates that Frédégonde was the king's mistress before his second marriage.  She plotted actively in favour of her sons at the expense of her husband's children by his fir
2 CONC st marriage.  It is likely that she ordered the murder of her brother-in-law King Sigebert I.  According to the 8th century source, Liber Historiae Francorum, she murdered her husband after he discovered that she was having an aff
2 CONC air[325].  She was regent for her son King Clotaire II after his succession in 584.  Fredegar records that Frédégonde died "in the second year of the reign of Theodebert"[326].'''  
2 CONT King Chilperich II & his third wife had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.         RIGUNDIS ([569]-).  Gregory of Tours names "Princess Rigunth" when recounting that she sympathised when Gregory was accused of treachery by her mother[342].  Gregory names Rigunth as daughter of King Chilperich when rec
2 CONC ording her betrothal to Recared, son of King Leuvigild, and her voyage to Spain with a large retinue[343].  On learning of the death of her father, she was abandoned before returning to her mother's palace where she led a life of 
2 CONC debauchery[344].  Betrothed (early 584) to RECAREDO of the Visigoths, son of LEOVIGILDO King of the Visigoths & his first wife Theodosia --- (-Toledo mid-Jun or Dec 601).  He was elected to succeed his father in 586 as RECAREDO Ki
2 CONC ng of the Visigoths. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.         CHLODEBERT (-Soissons St Médard 580, bur Soissons St Crispin and St Crispinian).  Gregory of Tours names Chlodebert as the son of Chilperich & Frédégonde when recording his death in the church of St Médard, Soissons and
2 CONC  his burial "in the church of the holy martyrs Crispin and Crispinian"[345], the context of the passage dating the event to 580. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.         SAMSON (573-late 577).  Gregory of Tours names Samson as the younger son of Chilperich & Fredegund when recording his death after having a high temperature and diarrhoea "before completing his fifth year"[346], the cont
2 CONC ext of the passage dating the event to late 577. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.         DAGOBERT ([579/80]-580, bur Saint-Denis).  Gregory of Tours refers to (but does not name) a younger son of Chilperich & Fredegund when recording his hurried baptism while dying and his burial in Paris Saint-Denis[347], 
2 CONC the context of the passage dating the event to 580.  Fortunatus, dated to the late 6th century, wrote an epitaph to “Dagoberto…puerilis obis”, naming "Chlodovechi…proavi…Chilpericque patris, vel Fredegunde genus"[348]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.      THEODERICH ([582/83], chr Paris 18 Apr 583-early 584, bur Paris).  Gregory of Tours records the birth of a son to King Chilperich and his baptism at Easter with the name Theoderich by Ragnemod Bishop of Paris[349], dated 
2 CONC from the context to [582/83].  Gregory records his death from dysentery a year after his baptism and his burial in Paris[350]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 11.      CHLOTHACHAR [Clotaire] (Spring 584-[18] Oct 629, bur Paris, Saint-Germain-des-Prés).  Gregory of Tours records the birth of a son to King Chilperich[351], dated to early 584 from the context, although in a later passage i
2 CONC n which he names him Clotaire he records that he was four months old when his father died[352].  He succeeded his father in 584 as CLOTAIRE II King of the Franks, under the regency of his mother Queen Frédégonde. 
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#ChilpericIdied584B
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Fredegund or Fredegunda (also Latin Fredegundis or French Frédégonde; died 597) was the Queen consort of [https://www.geni.com/people/Chilp%C3%A9ric-I-King-of-the-Franks-at-Soissons/6000000005913016304?through=5544231254350042383 
2 CONC Chilperic I], the Merovingian Frankish king of Soissons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Originally a servant, Fredegund became Chilperic's mistress after he had murdered his wife and queen, Galswintha (c. 568). But Galswintha's sister, Brunhilda, in revenge against Chilperic, began a feud which lasted more than 40 ye
2 CONC ars.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fredegund is said to be responsible for the assassination of Sigebert I in 575 and made attempts on the lives of Guntram (her brother-in-law and the king of Burgundy), Childebert II (Sigebert's son), and Brunhilda.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the mysterious assassination of Chilperic (584), Fredegund seized his riches and took refuge in the cathedral at Paris. Both she and her surviving son, Clothar II, were protected by Guntram until he died in 592.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours depicts her as ruthlessly murderous and sadistically cruel; in his account, Fredegund perhaps has few rivals in monstrousness. Although she did not live to see it, her son's execution of Brunhilda bore the mark of
2 CONC  Fredegund's hatred: Clothar II had the old queen, now in her sixties, stretched in agony upon the rack for three entire days, then watched her meet her death chained between four horses that were goaded to the four points of the 
2 CONC compass, tearing her body asunder.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fredegund died c. 8 December 597 in Paris, France. The tomb of Frédégonde (d. 597) is a mosaic figure of marble and copper, situated in Saint Denis Basilica, having come from St. Germain-des-Prés.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fredegund has been proposed as one of many sources for the folk tale alternatively known as Cinderella, Aschenputtel, Cennerenolla or Cendrillion. In his book Cinderella: A Casebook folklorist Alan Dundes sites the following excer
2 CONC pt from History of the Franks by Gregory of Tours:
2 CONT
2 CONT     She was jealous of her own daughter, Rigunth, who continually declared that she should be mistress (probably, as Dalton Says, because Fredegund began life as a palace maid, while she was of royal blood, being a king's daughter
2 CONC ) in her place. Fredegund waited her opportunity and under the pretense of magnanimity took her to the treasure-room and showed her the King's jewels in a large chest. Feigning fatigue, she exclaimed "I am weary; put thou in thy h
2 CONC and, and take out what thou mayest find." The mother thereupon forced down the lid on her neck and would have killed her had not the servants finally rushed to her aid.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile picture: Chilperic I and Fredegund.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fredegund or Fredegunda (Latin: Fredegundis; French: Frédégonde; died 597) was the Queen consort of Chilperic I, the Merovingian Frankish king of Soissons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Originally a servant, Fredegund became Chilperic's mistress after he had murdered his wife and queen, Galswintha (c. 568). But Galswintha's sister, Brunhilda, in revenge against Chilperic, began a feud which lasted more than 40 ye
2 CONC ars.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fredegund is said to be responsible for the assassination of Sigebert I in 575 and made attempts on the lives of Guntram (her brother-in-law and the king of Burgundy), Childebert II (Sigebert's son), and Brunhilda.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the mysterious assassination of Chilperic (584), Fredegund seized his riches and took refuge in the cathedral at Paris. Both she and her surviving son, Clothar II, were protected by Guntram until he died in 592.
