2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT * http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=pkd&id=I5480He40a
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wikipedia page on Hermanafrid:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermanfrid
2 CONT
2 CONT Hermanfrid (also Hermanifrid or Hermanafrid; Latin: Hermenfredus) was the last independent king of the Thuringii. He was one of three sons of King Bessinus (or Bisinus) and the Lombard Menia (or Basina). His siblings were Baderic;
2 CONC  Radegund (the elder), married to the Lombard king Wacho; and Bertachar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hermanfrid married Amalaberga, daughter of Amalafrida who was the daughter of Theodemir, between 507 and 511. Amalberga was also the niece of Theodoric the Great. It is unclear when Hermanfrid became king, but he is called king (r
2 CONC ex thoringorum) in a letter by Theodoric dated to 507. He first shared the rule with his brothers Baderic and Bertachar, but later killed Bertachar in a battle in 529, leaving the young Radegund an orphan.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Gregory of Tours, Amalaberga now stirred up Hermanfrid against his remaining brother. Once she laid out only half the table for a meal, and when questioned about the reason, she told him "A king who owns only of half 
2 CONC of his kingdom deserved to have half of his table bare." Thus roused, Hermanfrid made a pact with the king of Metz, Theuderic I, to march against Baderic. Baderic was overcome by the Franks and beheaded, but Hermanfrid refused to 
2 CONC fulfill his obligations to Theuderic, which led to enmity between the two kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 531 or 532, Theuderic, his son Theudebert I, and his brother King Clotaire I of Soissons attacked the Thuringii. The Franks won a battle near the river Unstrut and took the royal seat at Scithingi (modern Burgscheidungen). Herm
2 CONC anfrid managed to flee, but the Franks captured his niece Radegund (see Venantius Fortunatus, De excidio Thoringae) and his nephews.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theuderic gave Hermanfrid safe conduct, ordered him to come to Zülpich, and gave him many gifts. While Hermanfrid talked with Theuderic, somebody pushed him from the town walls of Zülpich and he died. Gregory mentions that certain
2 CONC  people had ventured to suggest that Theuderic might have had something to do with it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Radegund was then forced to marry King Clotaire, while Hermanfrid's wife Amalaberga fled to the Ostrogoths with her children Amalafrid and Rodelinda. She was later captured by the Byzantine general Belisarius and sent to Constanti
2 CONC nople, where Amalafrid later became an imperial general and Rodelinda was married to the Lombard king Auduin.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Thuringian kingdom ended with Hermanfrid. The area east of the Saale river was taken over by Slavic tribes, north Thuringia by the Saxons.
2 CONT
2 CONT The fall of the Thuringian dynasty became the subject of numerous epic treatments, the best known of which is in the Rerum gestarum saxonicarum libri tres by Widukind of Corvey, a Saxon foundation myth written in 967. Rudolph of F
2 CONC ulda tells a related story.
2 CONT
2 CONT About the Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT The main source for this period is Gregory of Tours, who represents the Frankish viewpoint. Widukind is much later and has clearly incorporated mythical elements into his account. Procopius only mentions the events in passing as f
2 CONC ar as they affect Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermanfrid for more information.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Baderic, Baderich, Balderich or Boderic (c.480-c.529), son of Bisinus and Basina, was a co-king of the Thuringii. He and his brothers Hermanfrid and Berthar succeeded their father Bisinus. After Hermanfrid defeated Berthar in batt
2 CONC le, he invited King Theuderic I of Metz to help him defeat Baderic in return for half of the kingdom. Theuderic I agreed and Baderic was defeated and killed in 529. Hermanfrid became the sole king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other info
