1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Source: Iordanes Acta Getarum, 6th century
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ITALY,%20Kings%20to%20962.htm#Theodegotha
2 CONT
2 CONT THEODORIC, son of THEODEMIR King of the Ostrogoths in Pannonia & his concubine Ereleuva --- ([451][264]-Ravenna 30 Aug 526). Iordanes names "Theodericum" as son of Theodemir, in a later passage naming his mother "Erelieva concubin
2 CONC a"[265]. …
2 CONT
2 CONT Concubine: ---. Iordanes records that Theodoric's two daughters were born "ex concubina…in Moesia" before his marriage to Audofledis[284]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of the concubine of Theodoric is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Theodoric had two illegitimate daughters by his concubine:
2 CONT
2 CONT *2. THEODEGOTHA [Thiudigotho]. Iordanes names "unam…Thiudigoto et aliam Ostrogotho" as the two daughters of Theodoric born "ex concubina…in Moesia" before his marriage to Audofledis, specifying that they came to Italy and were mar
2 CONC ried "unam Alarico Vesegotharum et aliam Sigismundo Burgundzonorum"[302]. Procopius records that “regi Visigothorum Alarico” married "Theoderici…Theudichusam virginem filiam"[303]. married ([494][304]) ALARIC II King of the Visigo
2 CONC ths, son of EURIC King of the Visigoths & his wife --- (-killed in battle Poitiers 507).
2 CONT *3. OSTROGOTHO ([475/80]-before [520]). Iordanes names "unam…Thiudigoto et aliam Ostrogotho" as the two daughters of Theodoric born "ex concubina…in Moesia" before his marriage to Audofledis, specifying that they came to Italy and
2 CONC  were married "unam Alarico Vesegotharum et aliam Sigismundo Burgundzonorum"[305]. Her father arranged her marriage as part of his negotiations for an alliance with the Burgundians. According to Settipani[306], this marriage took 
2 CONC place soon after Theoderic arrived in Italy. Wolfram suggests[307] that Theodoric's alliance with the Burgundians was settled in 496. married ([494/96]) as his first wife, SIGISMUND of Burgundy, son of GONDEBAUD King of Burgundy &
2 CONC  his wife Caratena (-murdered 523, bur Agaune). He succeeded his father in 516 as SIGISMUND King of Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT [284] Iordanes Getarum, MGH Auct. ant. V.1, p. 131
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Mesia
2 CONT
2 CONT De Wikipedia, la enciclopedia libre
2 CONT
2 CONT Ubicación de Mesia en el Imperio romano. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mapa de los antiguos Balcanes romanos en el siglo IV.Mesia (Moesia en latín; Μοισία, Moisia en griego; Мизия, Miziya en búlgaro; Moesia en rumano; Мезија, Mezija en serbio) era una provincia del Imperio romano, correspondiente a l
2 CONC as regiones ribereñas con el Danubio de los actuales Estados de Serbia y Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fue conquistada en el año 28 a. C. por las legiones de Octavio, quien al año siguiente (27 a. C.) fue nombrado Augusto y proclamado emperador.
2 CONT
2 CONT Durante la reorganización provincial realizada por Diocleciano (284-305), Mesia fue dividida en Mesia I (o Moesia Margensis, al Oeste) y Mesia II (o Moesia Inferior, al Este), además de una provincia situada justo en medio llamada
2 CONC  Dacia. Esta última región no corresponde a la Dacia conquistada por Trajano (98-117), sino que fue nombrada así tras el abandono de esa provincia por Aureliano (270-275), y en ella se asentaron los colonos de la Dacia abandonada.
2 CONT
2 CONT Posteriormente, a raíz de la desastrosa derrota romana en Adrianópolis (378), gran parte de la Mesia, además de las provincias balcánicas y griegas, sufrirían la devastación a manos de los visigodos, principalmente. Durante la pri
2 CONC mera mitad del siglo V de nuestra era, fueron los hunos (bajo el mando de diferentes reyes) quienes hicieronn continuas incursiones en busca de botín en la Mesia, teniendo el Imperio Oriental que rendirles tributo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finalmente, la Mesia sufriría las oleadas invasoras de serbios y, sobre todo, búlgaros durante el siglo VII, perdiéndose para siempre para los bizantinos o romanos de Oriente.
2 CONT
2 CONT En la actualidad, Mesia ("Miziya", en búlgaro) es el nombre de uno de los barrios de la ciudad de Pleven, Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Location of Moesia in the Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Map of the ancient Roman Balkans in the 4th century.Mesia (Moesia in Latin; Μοισία, Moisia in Greek; Мизия, Miziya in Bulgarian; Moesia in Romanian; Мезија, Mezija in Serbian) was a province of the Roman Empire, corresponding to t
2 CONC he regions bordering the Danube of the current States of Serbia and Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was conquered in the year 28 BC. C. by the legions of Octavian, who the following year (27 BC) was named Augustus and proclaimed emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the provincial reorganization carried out by Diocletian (284-305), Moesia was divided into Moesia I (or Moesia Margensis, to the west) and Moesia II (or Moesia Inferior, to the east), plus a province located right in the mi
2 CONC ddle called Dacia. This last region does not correspond to the Dacia conquered by Trajan (98-117), but was named after the abandonment of that province by Aurelian (270-275), and the colonists of the abandoned Dacia settled there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, following the disastrous Roman defeat at Adrianople (378), much of Moesia, in addition to the Balkan and Greek provinces, would suffer devastation at the hands of the Visigoths, mainly. During the first half of the 5th cent
2 CONC ury AD, it was the Huns (under the command of different kings) who made continuous raids in search of loot in Moesia, with the Eastern Empire having to pay tribute to them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, Moesia would suffer the invading waves of Serbs and, above all, Bulgarians during the 7th century, being lost forever to the Byzantines or Eastern Romans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Today, Mesia ("Miziya" in Bulgarian) is the name of one of the neighborhoods in the city of Pleven, Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The Roman province of Moesia, mainly modern day Bulgaria and Serbia, was a stretch of mountainous terrain in the west and more fertile plains in the east. It bordered Macedonia and Thracia to the south, the Black Sea (Pontus Euxin
2 CONC us) to the east and shared the Danube as a border with Dacia in the North.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000201072771828&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT >Source: UNRV Roman History < [https://www.unrv.com/provinces/moesia.php link] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT “Iordanes Getarum,” MGH Auct. ant. V.1, p. 131 < [http://ia800303.us.archive.org/1/items/gothichistoryofj00jord/gothichistoryofj00jord.pdf PDF] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT 277.  LIV. The kings [of the Suavi], Hunimund and Alaric, fearing the destruction that had come upon the Sciri, next made war upon the Goths, relying upon the aid of the Sarmatians, who had come to them as auxili- aries with their
2 CONC  kings Beuca and Babai. They summoned the last remnants of the Sciri, with Edica and Hunuulf, their chieftains, thinking they would fight the more des- perately to avenge themselves. They had on their side the Gepidae also, as wel
2 CONC l as no small reenforcements from the race of the Rugi and from others gathered here and there. Thus they brought together a great host at
2 CONT 278 the river Bolia in Pannonia and encamped there.
