1 NOTE Took a vow of Viginity to avoid getting married.
2 CONT Became a nun.
2 CONT Then got married to general Flavius Marcianus.
2 CONT Flavius Marcianus recovered the eastern em[ore but his isolationism and Honorius' ineptitude left the western empire open to Attila the hun's scourges.
1 NOTE EMPRESS OF THE EAST 414-454
1 NOTE Pulcheria Roman empress, regent for her younger brother Theodosius II (Eastern Roman emperor 408-450) from 414 to about 416, and an influential figure in his reign for many years thereafter.  Arcadius (reigned 383-408) and his wif
2 CONC e, Eudoxia. Pulcheria assumed the regency upon her appointment as Augusta (empress) on July 4, 414, and the court she ruled was characterized by extreme piety and chastity. In 421 she arranged the marriage of Theodosius with Athen
2 CONC ais, who assumed the name Eudocia. But the two women quarreled about 440, and Eudocia in 443 withdrew permanently to Jerusalem. The grand chamberlain Chrysaphius then acquired the dominant influence over Theodosius. When this advi
2 CONC ser fell from power shortly before Theodosius' death (inJuly 450), Pulcheria again came into prominence. She selected Marcian as Theodosius' successor and agreed to become his nominal wife in order to preserve the Theodosian dynas
2 CONC ty. Oct. 25, 451, she attended the Council of Chalcedon and wasloudly acclaimed by the bishops assembled there. She builtseveral churches in Constantinople and left all her possessionsto the poor.
