2 CONT Dagobert was immortalized in the song Le bon roi Dagobert (The Good King Dagobert), a nursery rhyme featuring exchanges between the king and his chief adviser, Saint Eligius (Eloi in French). The satirical rhymes place Dagobert in
2 CONC  various ridiculous positions from which Eligius' good advice manages to extract him. The text, which probably originated in the 18th century, became extremely popular as an expression of the anti-monarchist sentiment of the Frenc
2 CONC h Revolution. Other than placing Dagobert and Eligius in their respective roles, it has no historical accuracy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1984 a 112 minutes long french-Italian comedy, Le bon roi Dagobert - The good king Dagobert- was made based on I. Dagobert. The movie is surprisingly realistic in showing the realities of early barbarian France. The soundtrack 
2 CONC was composed by Guido and Mauricio De Angelis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagobert was a serial monogamist.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Nanthild and they had the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT Clovis II, who inherited the rest of his kingdom at a young age when his father died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regintrud who married into the Bavarian Agilolfings, either Theodo, Duke of Bavaria or his son Duke in Salzburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also had a mistress named Ragintrudis and they had the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigebert III
2 CONT
2 CONT His other wives were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Wulfefundis
2 CONT
2 CONT Bertechildis
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT The Royal Ancestry Bible Royal Ancestors of 300 Colonial American Families by Michel L. Call (chart 2055 & 2058) ISBN 1-933194-22-7
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Rei da Austrasie (623-634) e Rei de Burgondie (629-639), Neustrie (629-639) e depois dos Francs (629-639)
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagoberto era o filho mais velho de Clotário II e Berthetrude (ou possivelmente Haldetrude). Clotário reinou sozinho sobre todos os francos desde 613, e Dagoberto se tornou rei da Austrásia quando seus nobres independentes exigira
2 CONC m um rei próprio. Em 623, Clotário instalou seu filho Dagoberto na Austrásia.Com a morte de seu pai em 629, Dagoberto herdou os reinos da Nêustria e da Borgonha. Seu meio irmão Cariberto, filho de Sichilde, reinvidicou a Nêustria 
2 CONC mas Dagoberto se opôs a ele. Brodulf, irmão de Sichilde, suplicou a Dagoberto em nome de seu jovem sobrinho, mas Dagoberto o assassinou e cedeu a seu jovem irmão a Aquitânia.Cariberto morreu em 632, e seu filho Chilperico foi assa
2 CONC ssinado por ordem de Dagoberto. Em 632, Dagoberto tinha a Aquitânia e a Borgonha firmemente sob seu domínio, tornando-se o mais poderoso rei merovíngio em muitos anos e o mais respeitado governante do Ocidente.Também em 632, os no
2 CONC bres da Austrásia se revoltaram sob a liderança do prefeito do palácio, Pepino de Landen. Em 634, Dagoberto aplacou os nobres rebeldes colocando seu filho de apenas três anos de idade, Sigeberto III, no trono, e através disso cede
2 CONC ndo o poder real no mais oriental dos seus reinos, justamente como seu pai tinha feito com ele onze anos antes.Como rei, Dagoberto fez de Paris sua capital. Durante seu reino, ele construiu Altes Schloss (Castelo Antigo) em Meersb
2 CONC urg (na atual Alemanha), que atualmente é o mais antigo castelo habitado desse país. Religioso devoto, Dagoberto também foi responsável pela construção da Basílica de Saint-Denis no lugar de um monastério beneditino em Paris.Em 63
2 CONC 1, Dagoberto liderou três exércitos contra Samo, rei eslavo, mas suas forças austrasianas foram derrotadas em Wogatisburg.Dagoberto morreu em 639, e foi o primeiro dos reis franceses a ser sepultado nas tumbas reais de Saint-Denis
2 CONC . Seu segundo filho, Clóvis II, de seu casamento com Nantilde, herdou o resto de seu reino muito jovem.O padrão de divisão e assassinato que caracterizou até mesmo o poderoso reinado de Dagoberto continuou no século seguinte até P
2 CONC epino o Breve finalmente depor o último rei merovíngio em 751, estabelecendo a dinastia carolíngia. Os reis-meninos merovíngios permaneceram governantes fantoches que herdavam o trono ainda crianças e viviam apenas o bastante para
2 CONC  gerar um herdeiro masculino ou dois, enquanto o poder real ficava nas mãos das famílias nobres (a velha nobreza) que exercia controle feudal sobre a maior parte das terras.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagoberto foi imortalizado na canção Le bon roi Dagobert (O bom rei Dagoberto), uma música infantil retratando as trocas entre o rei e seu principal conselheiro, Santo Elígio (Saint Eloi em francês). As rimas satíricas colocam Dag
2 CONC oberto em várias posições ridículas das quais os bons conselhos de Elígio conseguem retirá-lo. O texto, que provavelmente se originou no século XVIII, tornou-se extremamente popular como uma expressão do sentimento anti-monarquist
2 CONC a da Revolução Francesa. Exceto por colocar Dagoberto e Elísio em suas respectivas funções, a canção não tem nenhuma precisão histórica.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT I am not fully satisfied that this connection exists through his daughter named Regintrud, although he would be neat to have in a family background. As such, this is a weak connection (at least through his daughter).
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wikipedia page on Dagobert I:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dagobert_I
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagobert I (c. 603 – 19 January 639) was the king of Austrasia (623–634), king of all the Franks (629–634), and king of Neustria and Burgundy (629–639). He was the last Merovingian dynast to wield any real royal power. Dagobert wa
2 CONC s the first of the Frankish kings to be buried in the royal tombs at Saint Denis Basilica.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rule in Austrasia
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagobert was the eldest son of Chlothar II and Haldetrude (575–604). Chlothar II had reigned alone over all the Franks since 613. In 623, Chlothar was forced to make Dagobert king of Austrasia by the nobility of that region, who w
2 CONC anted a king of their own.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Chlothar II granted Austrasia to Dagobert, he initially excluded Alsace, the Vosges, and the Ardennes, but shortly thereafter the Austrasian nobility forced him to concede these regions to Dagobert. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The rule of a Frank from the Austrasian heartland tied Alsace more closely to the Austrasian court. Dagobert created a new duchy (the later Duchy of Alsace) in southwest Austrasia to guard the region from Burgundian or Alemannic e
2 CONC ncroachments and ambitions. The duchy comprised the Vosges, the Burgundian Gate, and the Transjura. Dagobert made his courtier Gundoin the first duke of this new polity that was to last until the end of the Merovingian dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT United rule
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of his father in 629, Dagobert inherited the Neustrian and Burgundian kingdoms. His half-brother Charibert, son of Sichilde, claimed Neustria but Dagobert opposed him. Brodulf, the brother of Sichilde, petitioned Dago
2 CONC bert on behalf of his young nephew, but Dagobert assassinated him and gave his younger sibling Aquitaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charibert died in 632 and his son Chilperic was assassinated on Dagobert's orders. By 632, Dagobert had Burgundy and Aquitaine firmly under his rule, becoming the most powerful Merovingian king in many years and the most respected
2 CONC  ruler in the West.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 631, Dagobert led three armies against Samo, the rulers of the Slavs, but his Austrasian forces were defeated at Wogastisburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rule in Neustria, from Paris
2 CONT
2 CONT Also in 632, the nobles of Austrasia revolted under the mayor of the palace, Pepin of Landen. In 634, Dagobert appeased the rebellious nobles by putting his three-year-old son, Sigebert III, on the throne, thereby ceding royal pow
2 CONC er in the easternmost of his realms, just as his father had done for him 11 years earlier.
2 CONT
2 CONT As king, Dagobert made Paris his capital. During his reign, he built the Altes Schloss in Meersburg (in modern Germany), which today is the oldest inhabited castle in that country. Devoutly religious, Dagobert was also responsible
2 CONC  for the construction of the Saint Denis Basilica, at the site of a Benedictine monastery in Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagobert died in the abbey of Saint-Denis and was the first Frankish king to be buried in the Saint Denis Basilica, Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT The pattern of division and assassination which characterise even the strong king Dagobert's reign continued for the next century until Pepin the Short finally deposed the last Merovingian king in 751, establishing the Carolingian
2 CONC  dynasty. The Merovingian boy-kings remained ineffective rulers who inherited the throne as young children and lived only long enough to produce a male heir or two, while real power lay in the hands of the noble families who exerc
2 CONC ised feudal control over most of the land.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagobert was immortalized in the song Le bon roi Dagobert (The Good King Dagobert), a nursery rhyme featuring exchanges between the king and his chief adviser, Saint Eligius (Eloi in French). The satirical rhymes place Dagobert in
2 CONC  various ridiculous positions from which Eligius' good advice manages to extract him. The text, which probably originated in the 18th century, became extremely popular as an expression of the anti-monarchist sentiment of the Frenc
2 CONC h Revolution. Other than placing Dagobert and Eligius in their respective roles, it has no historical accuracy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1984, a 112-minute long French-Italian comedy, Le bon roi Dagobert (Good King Dagobert) was made, based on Dagobert I. The movie is surprisingly realistic in showing the realities of early barbarian France. The soundtrack was c
2 CONC omposed by Guido and Maurizio De Angelis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagobert was a serial monogamist. He married Nanthild and they had the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Clovis II, who inherited the rest of his kingdom at a young age when his father died.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Regintrud who married into the Bavarian Agilolfings, either Theodo, Duke of Bavaria or his son Duke in Salzburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also had a mistress named Ragintrudis (Ragnetrude) and they had the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Sigebert III
2 CONT
2 CONT His other wives were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Wulfefundis (Wulfegunde)
2 CONT
2 CONT Bertechildis (Berthilde)
2 CONT
2 CONT Gomentrude
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Frankish king, son and successor of King Clotaire II. His father was forced to appoint Dagobert king of the East Frankish kingdom of Austrasia at the request of Pepin of Landen, mayor of the palace, and Arnulf, bishop of Metz, who
2 CONC  effectively ruled in Austrasia. After Clotaire's death (629) Dagobert reunited Aquitaine with Austrasia and Neustria and became king of all the Franks. He was, however, forced by popular demand to give (634) Austrasia its own kin
2 CONC g in the person of his son, Sigebert III. The last of the Merovingians to exercise personal rule, he made himself independent of the great nobles, especially of Pepin of Landen. He extended his rule over the Basques and the Breton
2 CONC s. Dagobert's reign was prosperous; he was a patron of learning and the arts. He founded the first great abbey of Saint-Denis, where he is buried.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagobert I (died 639), king of the Franks (629-639). He became king of Austrasia in 623, and by 632 he had also brought Bourgogne and Aquitaine under his rule, becoming the most powerful of the Merovingian kings. He made Paris his
2 CONC  capital. Dagobert built numerous monasteries and strengthened the royal power.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dagobert_I
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Den sista frankiska kungen av den merovingiska dynastin att pröva ett rike förenas i mer än namnet. Son till Chlotar II blev Dagobert kung över Austrasien i 623 och hela frankiska riket i 629. Dagobert säkrade sitt rike genom att 
2 CONC göra en vänskap fördrag med den bysantinske kejsaren Herakleios och besegrade Gascons och Bretagne, och kampanjer mot slaverna på sin östra gräns. I 631 han skickade en armé till Spanien för att hjälpa visigotisk usurpatorn Swinth
2 CONC ila (Svintila). Han flyttade sin huvudstad från Austrasien till Paris, en central plats från vilken riket skulle styras mer effektivt. Han blidkade sedan Austrasians genom att göra sin tre-årige son Sigibert deras kung i 634. Berö
2 CONC md för sin kärlek för rättvisa, var Dagobert ändå girig och utsvävande. Han efterträddes av Sigibert III och en annan son Klodvig II. Välstånd Dagobert regeringstid, och ett återupplivande av konst under denna period, kan bedömas 
2 CONC från de rika innehållet i gravarna av perioden och från Guld-arbete för kyrkorna. Dagobert reviderade frankiska rätt, uppmuntras att lära, nedlåtande konst, och grundade den första stora klostret i Saint-Denis, som han gjort många
2 CONC  gåvor.
2 CONT
2 CONT För att nämna denna sida: "Dagobert I" Encyclopædia Britannica <http://www.britannica.com/eb/article?eu=28983&tocid=0&query=dagobert>
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Dagobert I was the King of Austrasia (623–634), King of all the Franks (629–634), and King of Neustria and Burgundy (629–639). He was the last Merovingian dynast to wield any real royal power. Dagobert was the first of the French 
2 CONC kings to be buried in the royal tombs at Saint Denis Basilica.
2 CONT
2 CONT As king, Dagobert made Paris his capital. During his reign, he built the Altes Schloss in Meersburg (in modern Germany), which today is the oldest inhabited castle in that country. Devoutly religious, Dagobert was also responsible
2 CONC  for the construction of the Saint Denis Basilica, at the site of a Benedictine monastery in Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagobert was a serial monogamist. He first married our ancestor Nanthild and they had the following two children, of whom one was our ancestor Clovis II. He also had a mistress named Ragintrudis (Ragnetrude) and he begat a son wit
2 CONC h her. His other wives were Wulfefundis (Wulfegunde), Bertechildis (Berthilde), and Gomentrude.
2 CONT
2 CONT The pattern of division and assassination which characterise even the strong king Dagobert's reign continued for the next century until Pepin the Short finally deposed the last Merovingian king in 751, establishing the Carolingian
2 CONC  dynasty. The Merovingian boy-kings remained ineffective rulers who inherited the throne as young children and lived only long enough to produce a male heir or two, while real power lay in the hands of the noble families who exerc
2 CONC ised feudal control over most of the land.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagobert was immortalized in the song Le bon roi Dagobert (The Good King Dagobert), a nursery rhyme featuring exchanges between the king and his chief adviser, Saint Eligius (Eloi in French). The satirical rhymes place Dagobert in
2 CONC  various ridiculous positions from which Eligius' good advice manages to extract him. The text, which probably originated in the 18th century, became extremely popular as an expression of the anti-monarchist sentiment of the Frenc
2 CONC h Revolution. Other than placing Dagobert and Eligius in their respective roles, it has no historical accuracy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1984, a 112-minute long French-Italian comedy, Le bon roi Dagobert (Good King Dagobert) was made, based on Dagobert I. The movie is surprisingly realistic in showing the realities of early barbarian France.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dagobert_I for more information.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Dagobert was our ancestor through two distinct lines of descent: through his son Clovis and his daughter Regintrude, each of whom was independently our ancestor.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Roi des Francs 
2 CONT
2 CONT Roi d'Austrasie
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Dagobert I (c. 603 - January 19, 639) was the king of the Franks from 629 to 639.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of King Clotaire II, Dagobert became king of Austrasia and on the death of his father, the sole king of the Franks. By 632 he had Bourgogne and Aquitaine under his rule, becoming the most powerful of the Merovingian kings 
2 CONC and the most respected ruler in the West. He married five times.
2 CONT
2 CONT As king, Dagobert I made Paris his capital. During his reign, he built the Altes Schloss Castle in Meersburg, Germany which today is the oldest inhabited castle in that country. Devoutly religious, Dagobert was also responsible fo
2 CONC r the construction of the Saint Denis Basilica at the site of a Benedictine Monastery in Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagobert was the last of the Merovingian kings to wield any real royal power. In 632 the nobles of Austrasia revolted under Mayor of the Palace Pepin I, and Dagobert appeased the rebellious nobles by putting his three-year-old son
2 CONC  Sigebert III on the Austrasian throne, thereby ceding royal power in all but name. When Dagobert died in 639, another son, Clovis II, inherited the rest of his kingdom at age five.
2 CONT
2 CONT This pattern continued for the next century until Pippin III finally deposed the last Merovingian king in 731, establishing the Carolingian dynasty. The Merovingian boy-kings remained ineffective rulers who inherited the throne as
2 CONC  young children and lived only long enough to produce a male heir or two, while real power lay in the hands of the noble families (the Old Noblesse) who exercised feudal control over most of the land.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagobert was the first of the French kings to be buried in the Royal tombs at Saint Denis Basilica.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Dagobert was immortalized by the song The good king Dagobert.
2 CONT
