2 CONT St. Sexburga, Abbess of Ely
2 CONT *(AD 636-c.700)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sexburga was the eldest daughter of King Anna of East Anglia and his second wife, Saewara: one of four saintly sisters. She married King Erconbert of Kent and became mother of the future Kings Egbert I and Hlothere, as well as SS.
2 CONC  Ermengilda and Ercongota. Further holy relatives included her grandchildren: St. Werburga of Chester, St. Wulfade & St. Rufinus. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Within her husband's lifetime, Sexburga began to build a religious house at Sheppey, in that kingdom, where holy virgins might attend divine service for her, day and night. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Erconbert died of the "yellow plague" that desolated England in AD 664 and, in widowhood, Sexburga was regent, for a time, on behalf of her son, Egbert I. When he had no further need of her, she retired to her nunnery and assemble
2 CONC d 74 nuns there. However, hearing of the great sanctity of her sister, Etheldreda of Ely, and desiring to live in more obscurity than she could enjoy as head of her own monastery, she chose to become a nun under the latter (before
2 CONC  AD 679). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, she succeeded her as Abbess of Ely and, during her rule, she translated her sister's body into an old Roman sarcophagus brought from nearby Grantchester. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She lived to a considerable age, dying on 6th July around AD 700.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edited from Agnes Dunbar's "A Dictionary of Saintly Women" (1904).
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seaxburh_of_Ely
2 CONT
