1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Wife of Ecgfrith. She was the daughter of
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna a Christian King of the East Angles and
2 CONT
2 CONT was born c630. Etheldreda was married twice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her first marriage was to a local
2 CONT
2 CONT prince/chieftain called Tondbert. He died three
2 CONT
2 CONT years after they were married and Etheldreda
2 CONT
2 CONT went to live on the Isle of Ely, which had been
2 CONT
2 CONT given to her by her father. There was a church
2 CONT
2 CONT there and she began to live a life of prayer
2 CONT
2 CONT very like that of a nun. In 658 another
2 CONT
2 CONT marriage was arranged for Etheldreda; this time to Ecgfrith, the son of the King of
2 CONT
2 CONT Northumbria. Although Etheldreda was married to Ecgfrith for 12 years it was not a
2 CONT
2 CONT success and she desperately wanted to live as a nun. Eventually Ecgfrith allowed Etheldreda
2 CONT
2 CONT to leave his Northumbrian court in 670 to become a nun. She received her veil and nun’s
2 CONT
2 CONT clothes from Wilfrid at a monastery at Coldingham where Ecgfrith’s aunt, Ebbe, was
2 CONT
2 CONT abbess. Ecgfrith became King of Northumbria shortly after this and tried to make
2 CONT
2 CONT Etheldreda return home. She fled the monastery at Coldingham and returned to Ely where
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilfrid joined her. Etheldreda had been given land by Ecgfrith when they were first married
2 CONT
2 CONT and this included land at Hexham. In 672 she gave this land to Wilfrid so that he could build
2 CONT
2 CONT a monastery. Etheldreda founded a double monastery at Ely (one for monks, one for nuns)
2 CONT
2 CONT and she was the abbess in charge of them both. Etheldreda lived there for seven years until
2 CONT
2 CONT she died from bubonic plague. She was buried in the monastic graveyard until her body was
2 CONT
2 CONT later moved inside the church. Etheldreda’s shrine can still be seen at Ely Cathedral today.
2 CONT --------------------
