2 _MARNM de Rodez
1 NAME Duke Ferreolus de Rodez
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 490
2 PLAC Moselle, Austrasia, France
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 528
2 ADDR
3 CITY Lorraine
3 STAE Moselle
3 CTRY France
1 OCCU Gallo-Roman Senator of Nabo (present Narbonne) and Rodez, Duke of the Moselle, Margrave of the Schelde
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was a Gallo-Roman Senator. Weis skips this generation. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferreolous seems to have been a grandson or son-in-law of Chlodéric (Chlodoreius). Either his mother was a daughter of Chlodéric or he was married to a daughter of Chlodéric.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page for Ferreolus of Rodez:  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferreolus_of_Rodez
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferreolus, also called Ferreolus of Rodez (b. 470 or ca 475) was a Senator of Narbonne, then Narbo, who lived in Rodez and was also a Senator there. He was the son of Tonantius Ferreolus and wife Industria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married firstly ca 531 to a Princess of the Salian Franks, born before 511, daughter of Chlodwig I, without issue, he later married secondly Saint Dode, born before 509, daughter of King Chloderic of the Ripuarian Franks, he was t
2 CONC he father of:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Ansbertus 2. Aigulf or Agilulf (b 537), Bishop of Metz (590 or 591 – 601) 3. Babon 4. Deotarius, Bishop of Arisitum 5. Ragenfred
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources and citations
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian Settipani, Les Ancêtres de Charlemagne (France: Éditions Christian, 1989).
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian Settipani, Continuite Gentilice et Continuite Familiale Dans Les Familles Senatoriales Romaines A L'epoque Imperiale, Mythe et Realite, Addenda I - III (juillet 2000- octobre 2002) (n.p.: Prosopographica et Genealogica, 
2 CONC 2002).
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT From the French Wikipedia page of his father, Tonantius Ferreolus (senateur): http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tonantius_Ferreolus_(s%C3%A9nateur)
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendance
2 CONT
2 CONT Les invasions barbares du ve siècle ne font pas disparaître d’un coup, les structures romaines de l’Occident. Les barbares ne représentent en effet que 5% de la population de l’Occident [7]. L’interdiction des mariages mixtes par 
2 CONC les Francs montre la peur de perdre leur identité. D’ailleurs leurs unions avec des femmes gallo-romaines restent relativement rares. Elles sont plus fréquentes avec les autres peuples qui envahissent l’empire. Les enfants de Tona
2 CONC nce II Ferréol et d’Industrie de Narbonne, sont [8]:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. probablement un fils peut-être nommé Ferréol, et sénateur dans la région de Narbonne, marié à une fille de Chlodéric, roi de Cologne [9], et père de : Ansbert, sénateur gallo-romain, se dévoue à la cause des rois d’Austrasie. D
2 CONC es généalogies carolingiennnes datant du ixe siècle lui attribuent pour épouse Bilichilde [10], fille de Clotaire Ier, mais cette parenté royale n'est plus prise en compte de nos jours [11].
2 CONT
2 CONT 1a. Agilulf, évêque de Metz de 591 à 601. 1b. Babon 1c. Déotaire, premier évêque d'Arisitum en 591. 1d. Ragenfred.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Firmin d'Uzès (516-553), 4e évêque d'Uzès de 507 à 553.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Fadence, père de:
2 CONT
2 CONT 3a. Saint Ferréol, 5e évêque d'Uzès de 553 à 581 [12].
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.↑ Michel Balard, Jean-Philippe Genêt, Michel Rouche, (1973), p 24 8.↑ Christian Settipani, Les Ancêtres de Charlemagne, Paris, 1989, 170 p. (ISBN 2-906483-28-1), p. 99-100. 9.↑ Voir l'article Origine des Arnulfiens pour les sour
2 CONC ces. 10.↑ la Genealogia domni Karoli, du début du ixe siècle et la Genealogia domni Arnulfi, du ixe siècle. 11.↑ Christian Settipani, Les Ancêtres de Charlemagne, Paris, 1989, 170 p. (ISBN 2-906483-28-1), p. 84. 12.↑ Cf. Dom Devic
2 CONC , dom Vaissète, Histoire générale de Languedoc
2 CONT
2 CONT In English:
2 CONT
2 CONT Progeny
2 CONT
2 CONT The barbarian invasions of the 5th century did not destroy all at once the structures of the western Roman Empire. The barbarians themselves represented only 5 percent of the population of the western parts of the empire [7]. The 
2 CONC prohibition of intermarriage by the Franks shows a fear of losing their identity. In any case, relations between Franks and Gallo-Roman women remained relatively rare. They were more common with other people who invaded the empire
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Tonance II Ferreolus and (Unknown) d'Industrie of Narbonne were [8]:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Probably a son, possibly named Ferreolus, who was senator for the region of Narbonne, who married a daughter of Chloderic, King of Cologne [9], and was the father of:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1a. Ansbert, Gallo-Roman senator who supported the cause of the Kings of Austrasia. Carolingian pedigrees dating from the 9th century ascribe as his wife Bilichilde [10], daughter of Clotaire I, but this connection to the royal fa
2 CONC mily is no longer taken into account [11]. 1b. Agilulf, Bishop of Metz (591-601) 1c. Babon 1d. Deotaire, first Bishop of Arisitum (591) 1e. Ragenfred
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Firmin d'Uzes, fourth bishop of Uzes (507/516-553)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Fadence, father of:
2 CONT
2 CONT 3a. St. Ferreol, 5th Bishop of Uzes (553-581)[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. ↑ Michel Balard, Jean-Philippe Genet, Michel Rouche (1973), p 24 8. ↑ Christian Settipani, "The Ancestors of Charlemagne", Paris, 1989, 170 p. (ISBN 2-906483-28-1), p. 99-100. 9. ↑ See the article "Origin of Arnulfiens" for sou
2 CONC rces. 10.↑ The "Genealogia domni Karoli" from the beginning of 9th century, and the "Genealogia domni Arnulfi" from the 9th century. 11.↑ Christian Settipani, "The Ancestors of Charlemagne", Paris, 1989, 170 p. (ISBN 2-906483-28-1
2 CONC ), p. 84. 12.↑ Cf Devic Dom Dom Vaissette, "General History of Languedoc"
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page of his father, Tonantius Ferreolus II (Senator): http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tonantius_Ferreolus_(senator)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferreolus, Senator of Narbonne, father of the Gallo-Roman Senator Ansbertus[8] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Settipani here cites Paul the Deacon in his work on the Bishops of Metz where Agilulf, Bishop of Metz, brother of Ansbert and uncle of Arnoald Bishop of Metz, was referred to as the "son of a senator". Metz was in the Kingdom of A
2 CONC ustrasia and Austrasia controlled Provence which included Uzes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Tonantius Ferreolus who was attested at Narbo likely took the side of the Goths before the death of Alaric II, by the mid 6th century his family had clearly relocated to within Frankish territory which began west of Uzes 
2 CONC and extended West. Nîmes, just to the south and a little west of Uzes was in Visigothic hands until the Arab capture in the 8th century. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Settipani, based on his reading of Paul the Deacon and the fact that the name Ferreolus was associated with the name Ansbert in two Autun Bishops (19 and 15 rspectively) in a Burgundian see that was regarded as both being heredita
2 CONC ry and having ties with the Syagri-Ferreoli, was persuaded apparently to accept the slightly confused 9th century account stating that the senator in question was a "Ferreolus." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Settipani suggests this Ferreolus tentatively as a son of Tonantius Ferreolus and Industria. Settipani further suggests that this son married to a daughter of Frankish Ripuarian Royal house which had survived through the clemency 
2 CONC of Theoderic of Austrasia who was thought to have been a son of Clovis' 1st wife, an unattested daughter of Sigebert, the penultimate Ripuarian Frankish king. Kelley had come to the same or a similar conclusion in 1947 [9] but it 
2 CONC appears from those who cite him that the original idea was that Ansbertus was a son of Tonantius Ferreolus and not a grandson.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Also called Tonance Ferréol.3 Tonantius II Ferreolus, sénateur Narbonne was connected with the Aviti through his mother, Papianilla.4 He was born say 450. He was the son of Tonantius I Ferreolus, praefectus praetorio Galliarum and
2 CONC  Papianille, clarissima femina. He was on the estates of his father when Sidonius Appolinarius visited. As Sidonius relates, "at Prusianum, as the other [estate] is called, [the young] Tonantius and his brothers turned out of thei
2 CONC r beds for us because we could not be always dragging our gear about: they are surely the elect among the nobles of our own age" between 461 and 467.5 He was present in 469 in Rome, Italy. He married Industria of Narbonne, daughte
2 CONC r of Flavius Probus and Eulalie (?), before 475.6 He was again present in 475 in Rome, Italy. Senator of Narbonne in Gaul between 479 and 517.3,6 Vir clarissimus between 507 and 511.6 He was visited by his cousin St. Apollinaris o
2 CONC f Valence in 517.6 He died after 517.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Note: Duke of the Moselle and Margrave of the Schelde, m. (1) a dau. foClovis, I, King of France, and m (2) Deuteria, a Roman Lady, and hadby his second wife, Ausbert. (YFT, p. 67). 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferreolus,_Senator_of_Narbonne
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ferreolus, also called Ferreolus of Rodez (b. 470 or ca 475) was a Senator of Narbonne, then Narbo, who lived in Rodez and was also a Senator there. He was the son of Tonantius Ferreolus and wife Industria. Married firstly ca 531 
2 CONC to a Princess of the Salian Franks, born before 511, daughter of Chlodwig I, without issue, he later married secondly Saint Dode, born before 509, daughter of King Chloderic of the Ripuarian Franks, he was the father of: ▪ Ansbert
2 CONC us
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ferreolus, Duke of Moselle
2 CONT
2 CONT b.abt.465 of Moselle, Austrasia, France; s/o Gigimerus I, Bishop of Auvergne and Miss Tonatius m.Outeria/Deuteria, Duchess of Moselle CHILDREN included: Tonatius Ferroleus of Gaul Ausbert b.abt.536
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT According to the Wikipedia page on his father Tonantius Ferreolus:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tonantius_Ferreolus_(senator)
2 CONT
2 CONT Fidentius, the father of: 
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Ferreolus, Bishop of Uzès (b. 530; succeeded his uncle in 553 ; d. January 4, 581, spent much of the early days of his term converting Jews in his diocese), Feast Day January 4, and 
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Tarsicia of Rodez (died ca 600), Feast Day January 15 (who lived as a hermit in the Rouergue near Rodez) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mommarets and Kelley [18] say that Ferreolus Bishop of Uzes was son of a Ferreolus, presumably Ferreolus, Senator of Narbonne above.
