1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/André_Dauphin_de_Bourgogne
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''André, Dauphin of Burgundy''' , born around 1184, died on March 14, 1237, Dauphin of Vienne, Count of Albon in 1203, then Count of Vienne from 1204 to 1237, known — wrongly — as Guigues  VI or Guigues  VI Dauphin (sometimes as 
2 CONC Guigues-André ), son of Hugues  III , Duke of Burgundy , and Beatrice of Albon , Countess of Albon and Viennois.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Guigues  VI is an incorrect name given to him much later by some historians. In all his acts, he signs André Dauphin.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guigues_VI_of_Viennois
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew Guigues VI (1184 – 14 March 1237), known as André de Bourgogne, Dauphin of Viennois, was the Count of Albon, Briançon, Grenoble, and Oisans from 1228 until his death. He was the son of Hugh III of Burgundy and Béatrice of A
2 CONC lbon. He took his regnal name after and inherited the titles and lands of his maternal grandfather, Guigues V.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his reign he was a generous patron of monasteries and he expanded his territory by diplomacy rather than war. He founded the collegiate church Saint-André of Grenoble, which is today the last existing monument built by the 
2 CONC delphinal dynasty, and where he and some of his successors were buried.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1202 he married Beatrice (1182 – before 1248), Countess of Gap and Embrun, daughter of Rainon I of Sabran. Their daughter Beatrice (born 1205) married Amaury de Montfort. In 1215 they separated and on 15 November 1219 Guigues m
2 CONC arried Beatrice, daughter of William VI of Montferrat. She was the domna (lady) of the troubadour Gauseran de Saint Leidier. She bore Guigues two sons: Guigues VII (1225–1269) and John (1227–1239).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT D.      COMTES d’ALBON, DAUPHINS (BOURGOGNE [CAPET])
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/burgkvien.htm#GuiguesVIdied1237 
2 CONT
2 CONT The county of Albon passed by marriage to a younger branch of the family of the dukes of Burgundy.  As noted in the Introduction to Part C above, the term “dauphin” was used as a type of patronymic by André de Bourgogne [Capet], s
2 CONC on of Beatrix heiress of Viennois and granddaughter of Guigues [VI], to highlight his maternal descent[294].  By the end of the 13th century, “dauphin” had evolved into a title borne by the rulers of Albon and Viennois.  The first
2 CONC  reference to the "delphinatum" of Vienne and Albon dates to 1285[295].  In addition, it was transformed into a regular name in the family of the comtes d’Auvergne, into which it was transmitted through Marquise, daughter of Guigu
2 CONC es [VI][296]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''ANDRE de Bourgogne''', son of HUGUES III Duke of Burgundy & his second wife Beatrix de Viennois Ctss d'Albon (1184-14 Mar 1237, bur Grenoble, église collégiale de Saint-André).  The Aymari Rivalli De Allobrogibus names "Octaviu
2 CONC m et Andream" as the two sons of "Beatrice relictis Tallifer" and "Hugo Burgundiæ dux"[297].  "Hugo Burgundie dux et Albonii comes" donated property to the Templars at Beaune, with the support of "Beatricis uxoris mee et…filiorum 
2 CONC meorum Odonis, Alexandri et Dalphini", by charter dated Dec 1188[298].  Comte de Gap et d'Embrun by right of his first wife in 1202.  He succeeded his mother in 1228 as Comte d’Albon, and was also referred to as "Dauphin".  A char
2 CONC ter dated 1210 records an agreement between "dominus Andreas Dalfinus" and the church of Embrun, relating to donations by "domino Wilelmo bonæ memoriæ comite Forcalcarii" and "eidem Delfino et uxori eius"[299].  "Andreas comes Alb
2 CONC ionensis" donated property to Léoncel by charter dated 1227[300].  "Andreas Dalphinus comes Vienne et Ailbonis" donated property to Léoncel by charter dated 4 Jul 1231[301].  The testament of "Dom. Andreas Dalphinus Viennensis et 
2 CONC Albonis comitis" is dated 4 Mar 1236, names as his heir "Guigonem filium suum", appoints "Beatricem uxorem suam comitissam matris eiusdem Guigonis" as his tutor, and names "Beatrici filiæ suæ uxori comitis Montis-fortis"[302].  Th
2 CONC e Aymari Rivalli De Allobrogibus records the death "III Id Mar" in 1237 of "Andreas Delphinus"[303]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' firstly (Saint-Firmin-sur-le-Buech Jun 1202, separated for consanguinity 1211) BEATRIX de Sabran, daughter and heiress of RAINON [I] de Sabran Seigneur de Caylar et d'Ansouis & his wife Garsende de Forcalquier Ctss de Gap 
2 CONC et d'Embrun (1182-after 5 Sep 1215).  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' secondly ([1216]) SEMNORESSE de Poitiers, daughter of AYMAR [II] de Poitiers Comte de Valentinois & his wife Philippa de FayHer marriage is shown in Europäische Stammtafeln[307].  Kerrebrouck[308] says that it is incorrect
2 CONC , but does not give the basis for his doubts. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' thirdly (21 Nov 1219) as her first husband, BEATRICE di Monferrato, daughter of GUGLIELMO VI Marchese di Monferrato & his [second] wife Berta di Clavesana ([1204/10]-after 4 Mar 1236). 
2 CONT
2 CONT André & his first wife had [two] children:
2 CONT *1.         BEATRIX de Viennois ([1205]-17 Sep after 1248).  … Betrothed (1207) to GUILLAUME de Nevers, son of HERVE [IV] Seigneur de Donzy Comte de Nevers & his wife Mathilde de Courtenay Ctss de Nevers, d'Auxerre et de Tonnerre 
2 CONC (-[1207/12]).  m (Betrothed Carcassonne [1214], consummated 1222) AMAURY [VII] de Montfort, son of SIMON [V] de Montfort Earl of Leicester, Duc de Narbonne, Comte de Toulouse, Vicomte de Béziers et de Carcassonne, Seigneur de Mont
2 CONC fort & his wife Alix de Montmorency (-Otranto [Apr] 1241, bur Rome, St Peter's).  He succeeded his father in 1218 as Duc de Narbonne, Comte de Toulouse, Seigneur de Montfort.  He later used the title Earl of Leicester. 
2 CONT * 2.    [uncertain]     [MARGUERITE de Viennois ([1203/07]-[1242]). … m (before 1221) as his [second] wife, AMEDEE de Savoie, son of THOMAS I Comte de Savoie & his wife Marguerite [Béatrix] de Genève (Montmélian, Savoie 1197-Montm
2 CONC élian 24 Jun or 13 Jul 1253, bur Hautecombe, abbaye royale de Sainte-Marie).  He succeeded his father in 1233 as AMEDEE IV Comte de Savoie.]
2 CONT
2 CONT André & his third wife had [two] children:
2 CONT *3.         GUIGUES de Viennois (1225-Aug 1269, bur Chartreuse Abbaye de Prémol). … Betrothed (contract [Jan/May] 1240) to CECILE des Baux, daughter of BARRAL Seigneur de Baux & his wife Béatrice d'Anduze (-21 May 1275).  … Betrot
2 CONC hed (contract before Jun 1240, annulled Jun 1240) to SANCHA de Provence, daughter of RAYMOND BERENGER IV Comte de Provence & his wife Beatrix de Savoie (Aix-en-Provence [1225]-Berkhamstead Castle, Buckinghamshire 5 or 9 Nov 1261, 
2 CONC bur Hayles Abbey, Gloucestershire).  … m (Betrothed 3 Dec 1241, [before 22 Apr 1253]) as her first husband, BEATRIX de Savoie Dame de Faucigny, daughter and heiress of PIERRE Comte de Savoie, Duc de Chablais & his wife Agnès Dame 
2 CONC de Faucigny 
2 CONT *4.          [JEAN de Viennois (1227-1239).  Ludovico della Chiesa’s Histoire de Piedmont names "Guigo quarto et Gioanni mori intorno al 1239" as the sons of André Comte d’Albon and his wife Beatrice di Monferrato[348].  The prima
2 CONC ry source on which this information is not cited, and has not yet been identified.  It is not known how accurate this information might be, but until more definite corroboration comes to light, Jean is included here in square brac
2 CONC kets.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT *https://familypedia.wikia.org/wiki/Andr%C3%A9_Guigues_VI_du_Viennois_(1184-1237)
2 CONT *http://cookancestry.com/web/glenn%20cook%20master%20file/11507.htm
