2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Amadeus was born at Le Bourget-du-Lac, and succeeded his childless paternal uncle Philip I, Count of Savoy as Count of Savoy in 1285. Amadeus was a younger brother of Thomas III of Piedmont, who had died in 1282. Thomas had been s
2 CONC ucceeded by his eldest son Philip I of Piedmont who had a stronger claim to the County than Amadeus. However Philip was about seven years old and at the time unable to press a claim.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amadeus managed to secure the support of his nephew by offering Philip control of Turin and Pinerolo. Amadeus also secured the loyalty of his younger brother Louis by offering him Vaud as an hereditary barony.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 October 1285, Amadeus was declared protector of Geneva after negotiations with the Bishop of Geneva. The hereditary title belonged to Amadeus II, Count of Geneva who was in conflift with the Bishop.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through his marriage to Sybilla, Countess of Bugey and Bresse, he was able to incorporate these Burgundian districts into his states. Later expansion saw his dominions further increased.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1295, Amadeus acquired the Chambéry fortress from its previous owner Hugh of La Rochette. He chose Chambéry as his main residence and in the following decorated it with paintings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among his successes was the Treaty of Annemasse which the Count of Geneva and the Dauphin of Viennois accepted subservient roles to him as his vassals. The treaty was the result of military victories over the both of them. In 1301
2 CONC , Amadeus also settled his dispute over control of Valais with the Roman Catholic Diocese of Sion.
2 CONT
2 CONT His reign, however, also saw friction between the County of Savoy and the Duchy of Austria. He pursued an alliance with the Kingdom of France and received Maulévrier in Normandy as a result of initial good relations.
2 CONT
2 CONT The eventual recovery of Lyon by the Kings of France alerted Amadeus to their expansionistic tendencies towards the regions by the Alps. He sought a powerful ally against potential hostility in Henry VII, Holy Roman Emperor. Henry
2 CONC  was married to Margaret of Brabant, sister-in-law of Amadeus. The relation through marriage probably helped the alliance. Henry awarded Amadeus with the title of imperial vicar for the Kingdom of Italy and at least titular claim 
2 CONC over the Asti region.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1315, Amadeus assisted the Knights Hospitaller in the defense of Rhodes against the Turks.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family and children
2 CONT
2 CONT He first married Sybille of Bage and had 8 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Bonne of Savoy, married John I of Viennois, dauphin of Viennois and Hugh of Burgundy, lord of Montbauson
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. John of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Beatrice of Savoy, married Manfred III of Saluzzo
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Edoardo
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Eleonor of Savoy, married William of Chalon, count of Auxerre and Tonnerre, Dreux IV of Mello and John I, count Forez - their children included Marguerite of Mello, who married John II of Chalon-Arlay
2 CONT
2 CONT    6. Margaret of Savoy, d. 1349, married to John I of Montferrat
2 CONT
2 CONT    7. Agnes of Savoy, d. 1372, married to William III of Geneva
2 CONT
2 CONT    8. Aimone
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, secondly, Maria of Brabant who was a daughter of John I, Duke of Brabant and Margaret of Flanders. Her maternal grandparents were Guy of Dampierre and his first wife Matilda of Bethune. They had 4 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Maria of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Catherine of Savoy, d. 1336, married to Leopold I (duke of Austria and Styria)
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Anna of Savoy, d. 1359, married to Byzantine Emperor Andronikos III Palaiologos
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Beatrice of Savoy (1310-1331), married in 1327 to Henry VI, Duke of Carinthia, count of Tirol
2 CONT
2 CONT External links
2 CONT
