1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan_of_Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan of Savoy also known as Giovanna di Savoia (1310 – 29 June 1344), was Duchess consort of Brittany, wife of John III, Duke of Brittany.[1] Joan was also a claimant to the County of Savoy upon the death of her father. She was a 
2 CONC member of the House of Savoy and married into the House of Dreux. Joan was born in 1310, she was the only child of Edward, Count of Savoy and his wife Bianca of Burgundy.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan married in 1329 aged nineteen to the forty-three-year-old, childless John III, Duke of Brittany; she was his third wife, John's second wife Isabella had died the previous year.
2 CONT
2 CONT The same year as Joan's marriage, her father died. Being his only child she considered herself his successor. However, Savoy had never had a female ruler, leading to a dispute in the succession. Joan's uncle Aymon had the support 
2 CONC of the nobles of Savoy for the Semi-Salic inheritance and succeeded as Count.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT John supported Joan's rights on Savoy. After the marriage, Joan renewed her claim on Savoy and allied herself with the Dauphin de Viennois against her uncle.[2] By agreement settled by the French King [3] on 22 November 1339, she 
2 CONC renounced her rights of succession in return for an annual income of 6000 livres.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan and John were married for twelve years but produced no offspring, and John died on 30 April 1341, leaving Joan a childless widow. This led to a disputed succession in Brittany between John's half-brother of the same name and 
2 CONC John's niece Joan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1343, when her uncle Aymon died, Joan renewed her claim on the county of Savoy against her nine-year-old cousin, Amadeus VI. In her will, she left the county to Philip, Duke of Orléans to spite her cousins. In the end, he negot
2 CONC iated a similar settlement to the one of Joan, yielding the claim in exchange for 5000 livres annually.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan died on 29 June 1344.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT # Cox 1967, p. 22.
2 CONT # Cox 1967, p. 53-55.
2 CONT
2 CONT #    State Archives, volume 104, page 51, fascicule 9.1, and Guichenon (Savoie), Tome IV, Preuves, p. 170.
2 CONT
2 CONT  References
2 CONT
2 CONT *    Cox, Eugene L. (1967). The Green Count of Savoy. Princeton, New Jersey: Princeton University Press. LCCN 67-11030.
