2 DATE 21 AUG 1464
1 OCCU chevalier, seigneur d’Estouteville, Valmont et Hotot, gouverneur de Normandie, capitaine de Harfleur (1425-1460), capitaine du Mont Saint-Michel de Rouen (1461-1464), Grand-Bouteiller de France (1435), Lieutenant-Général puis Gran
2 CONC d-Sénéchal de Norma
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} from MEDLANDS 
2 CONT
2 CONT LOUIS d’Estouteville, son of JEAN [II] Seigneur d’Estouteville & his wife Marguerite d’Harcourt Dame de Longueville et de Plaines (-21 Aug 1464)).  Seigneur d’Estouteville, de Vallemont et de Hotot. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([1414/15]?) JEANNE Paynell Dame de Moyon, de Briquebec et de Gascé, daughter of NICOLAS Paynel Seigneur de Hambye & his wife Jeanne de Champagne Dame de Gascé (-[1456]).  Le Cacheux records her parentage and marriage[388].  Dam
2 CONC e de Hambye 1432.  Louis & his wife had children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         MICHEL d’Estouteville (-after 1465).  Seigneur d’Estouteville, de Vallemont et de Hotot.  m as her first husband, MARIE de la Roche-Guyon Dame de la Roche-Guyon, d’Acquigny, d’Attichy, d’Auneau, de Bernarville, de Roche
2 CONC fort, de Roncheville et de Vaux, daughter of GUY Seigneur de la Roche-Guyon & his wife Catherin Turpin.  Her family origin and first marriage are indicated by the following document: [her aunt] “Marguerite de la Roche-guyon veuve 
2 CONC de feu messire Iean de Vergy Seigneur de Fonuans et de Vignory”, wishing to leave “[le] pays de Langres” for Normandy, requested “meisseigneurs Iaques, Guyon et Iean dits d’Estouteuille chevaliers...lesdits...Iaques et Guyon...com
2 CONC me enfans de Madame sa niepce” permission to reside “avec eux en leur place et chasteau de Hambuye”, which was agreed by “mondit Seigneur Guyon d’Estouteville pour luy...et pour lesdits Messires Iaques et Iean d’Estouteville ses f
2 CONC rere et oncle”, by charter dated 24 Feb 1479 (O.S.?)[389].  She married secondly Bertin de Silly Seigneur de Lonray.  Père Anselme records her parentage and two marriages, but provides no source citations[390].  Michel & his wife 
2 CONC had children:  a)         JACQUES d’Estouteville (-12 Mar 1489).  His parentage is indicated by the following document: “Marguerite de la Roche-guyon veuve de feu messire Iean de Vergy Seigneur de Fonuans et de Vignory”, wishing t
2 CONC o leave “[le] pays de Langres” for Normandy, requested “meisseigneurs Iaques, Guyon et Iean dits d’Estouteuille chevaliers...lesdits...Iaques et Guyon...comme enfans de Madame sa niepce” permission to reside “avec eux en leur plac
2 CONC e et chasteau de Hambuye”, which was agreed by “mondit Seigneur Guyon d’Estouteville pour luy...et pour lesdits Messires Iaques et Iean d’Estouteville ses frere et oncle”, by charter dated 24 Feb 1479 (O.S.?)[391].  Seigneur d’Est
2 CONC outeville, de Vallemont et de Hotot.  m (1480) JEANNE LOUISE d'Albret, previously mistress of of JEAN II "le Bon" Duc de Bourbon et d’Auvergne, daughter of JEAN d'Albret Seigneur d´Albret, Vicomte de Tartas & his wife Catherine de
2 CONC  Rohan (-8 Sep 1494).  Jacques & his wife had children:  i)          JEAN [III] d’Estouteville (1482-).  Seigneur d’Estouteville, de Vallemont et de Hotot.  m (1509) his first cousin, JACQUELINE d’Estouteville Dame de Moyon et de 
2 CONC Gascé, daughter of GUYON d’Estouteville Seigneur de Moyon, de Hambye, de Briquebec et de Gascé & his wife Isabelle de Croÿ.  Jean [III] & his wife had one child:  (a)       ADRIENNE d’Estouteville (20 Oct 1512-Trie [15/31] Dec 156
2 CONC 0, bur Vallemont).  Père Anselme records her marriage contract dated 9 Feb 1534 (O.S.?)[392].  Duchesse d’Estouteville.  m (contract Paris 9 Feb 1535) FRANÇOIS de Bourbon Comte de Saint-Pol, son of FRANÇOIS de Bourbon Comte de Ven
2 CONC dôme & his wife Marie de Luxembourg (Ham 6 Oct 1491-Cotignan near Reims 1 Sep 1545, bur abbaye de Vallemont).  Duc d’Estouteville.  ii)         FRANÇOISE d’Estouteville .  m as his second wife, JEAN [V] de Lévis Seigneur de Mirepo
2 CONC ix, son of JEAN [IV] de Lévis Seigneur de Mirepoix & his second wife Charlotte de Lévis (-1530 or after).  b)         GUYON d’Estouteville (-after 11 Nov 1505).  His parentage is indicated by the following document: “Marguerite de
2 CONC  la Roche-guyon veuve de feu messire Iean de Vergy Seigneur de Fonuans et de Vignory”, wishing to leave “[le] pays de Langres” for Normandy, requested “meisseigneurs Iaques, Guyon et Iean dits d’Estouteuille chevaliers...lesdits..
2 CONC .Iaques et Guyon...comme enfans de Madame sa niepce” permission to reside “avec eux en leur place et chasteau de Hambuye”, which was agreed by “mondit Seigneur Guyon d’Estouteville pour luy...et pour lesdits Messires Iaques et Iea
2 CONC n d’Estouteville ses frere et oncle”, by charter dated 24 Feb 1479 (O.S.?)[393].  Seigneur de Moyon, de Hambye, de Briquebec et de Gascé.  m ISABELLE de Croÿ, daughter of ANTOINE de Croÿ Comte de Porcien & his second wife Margueri
2 CONC te de Lorraine.  Guyon & his wife had one child:  i)          JACQUELINE d’Estouteville .  m (1509) her first cousin, JEAN d’Estouteville Seigneur d’Estouteville, son of JACQUES d’Estouteville Seigneur d’Estouteville, de Vallemont
2 CONC  et de Hotot & his wife Jeanne Louise d’Albret (1482-). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         JEAN d’Estouteville (-after 4 May 1476).  His family connection is indicated by the following document: “Marguerite de la Roche-guyon veuve de feu messire Iean de Vergy Seigneur de Fonuans et de Vignory”, wishing to lea
2 CONC ve “[le] pays de Langres” for Normandy, requested “meisseigneurs Iaques, Guyon et Iean dits d’Estouteuille chevaliers...lesdits...Iaques et Guyon...comme enfans de Madame sa niepce” permission to reside “avec eux en leur place et 
2 CONC chasteau de Hambuye”, which was agreed by “mondit Seigneur Guyon d’Estouteville pour luy...et pour lesdits Messires Iaques et Iean d’Estouteville ses frere et oncle”, by charter dated 24 Feb 1479 (O.S.?)[394].  Seigneur de Briqueb
2 CONC ec, de Hambye et de Gascé. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ———————————- From the tree of Alain GARRIC: https://gw.geneanet.org/garric?lang=fr&n=d+estouteville&oc=0&p=louis
2 CONT
2 CONT June 17, 1434. From the top of the walls, the Chevalier d'Estouteville contemplates in the setting sun the expanse of blood reddened sands. It is littered with two thousand corpses of English soldiers who, since dawn, had launched
2 CONC  an assault on the Mount.
2 CONT
2 CONT For thirty years the "godons" besieged the fortress.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis d'Estouteville has been in command of the Montoise resistance for nine years. He has at his side his wife, Jeanne Paisnel, daughter of the previous governor, Lord of Bricquebec, and one hundred and eighteen other knights who
2 CONC , like him, have sworn to keep this place for the King of France or to perish buried under its debris.
2 CONT
2 CONT At daybreak, twenty thousand English tried to rush into a breach opened by their formidable artillery. With energy, the knights and the thousand soldiers locked in the stronghold cut them to pieces and pursued on the strikes with 
2 CONC the cry of Saint-Michel. They know they have won a decisive battle. The English will forever give up seizing the Wonder.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hundred Years War is coming to an end. Louis d'Estouteville, who has already distinguished himself by taking Tombelaine and Granville, will soon bring down Saint-James and Avranches. In the meantime, his men recover the abando
2 CONC ned English cannons, two of which can still be seen at the entrance to the Mount.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mont-Saint-Michel had no strategic interest, but, since 1423, but was the symbol of resistance to the occupier.
2 CONT
2 CONT As early as 1427, the names of the knights had been engraved in the Basilica.
