1 OCCU Seigneur of Laval and of Kergorlay, Seigneur de Kergolay, depuis 1406 Sire de Laval et de Vitré
1 TITL Count
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guy_XIII_de_Laval
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy XIII de Laval, or before Jean de Montfort, (born in 1385, died on 14 August 1414 in Rhodes), lord of Kergorlay, Baron of La Roche-Bernard, baron of Laz, castellan of La Brétesche, lord of Lohéac, and of La Roche-en-Nort, then 
2 CONC by marriage, baron of Vitré, lord of Laval, and of Acquigny1, Viscount of Rennes, knight banneret.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Main article: Kergorlay family
2 CONT Son of Raoul IX de Montfort and Jeanne de Kergorlay
2 CONT married 22 January 1404 to Anne (1385 † 25 January 1466), lady heiress of Laval, baroness heiress of Vitré, viscountess heiress of Rennes, Châtillon, Gavre2, d'Acquigny, d'Aubigné, Courbeveille, lady heiress of Tinténiac, Bécherel
2 CONC  and Romillé, a marriage attended by several bishops and the greatest lords of Brittany3. From this marriage came:
2 CONT Guy XIV de Laval,
2 CONT André de Lohéac,
2 CONT Louis de Laval,
2 CONT Jeanne de Laval, married in 1424 to Louis Iof Bourbon-Vendôme,
2 CONT Catherine de Laval, wife of Guy III de Chauvigny, vicomte de Brosse,
2 CONT History
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT He married on 22 January 14044 with Anne de Laval, daughter and sole heiress of Guy XII de Laval; at this marriage were present several bishops and the greatest lords of Brittany3.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the conditions was that the children to be born of this marriage should bear the name and arms of Laval. The same rule had already been applied previously at the time of the marriage of Emma de Laval to Mathieu II de Montmo
2 CONC rency. However, Jean de Montfort himself renounced his name during his lifetime to take that of Guy XIII de Laval, in order to be associated with the power of his wife, Anne de Laval.
2 CONT
2 CONT Knight banneret
2 CONT For the Art of Checking Dates5, since his marriage he had served in the armies of France, under the name of Comte de Gavre, with the title of squire banneret6. He appeared at court under this title and followed the party of the Da
2 CONC uphin (future Charles VII of France) in the Civil War between the Armagnacs and the Burgundians. He had the command of six hundred men-at-arms, seven of whom were bachelors7, and one hundred and eighty squires8 who formed his comp
2 CONC any.
2 CONT
2 CONT He called himself Guy XIII de Laval on the death of his father-in-law in 14129.
2 CONT
2 CONT Holy Land
2 CONT Following the Peace of Bourges signed on July 15, 1412, he resolved to visit the Holy Land, and to pray at the Tomb of Christ in accordance with a vow. According to the custom of the ancient pilgrims, he invokes the favor of heave
2 CONC n on his perilous journey by pious foundations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before his departure, he declared, by letters of 31 August, that going on the journey overseas, he left to the Sire de Montfort, his father, and to the lady of Laval, his wife, full power to keep and govern his lands situated in B
2 CONC rittany, Anjou, Maine, Normandy, France, Picardy, Flanders, Hainaut, and Artois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Having set out accompanied by a retinue of gentlemen, he went straight to Palestine, and after satisfying his piety and devotion there, he took the boat by way of the island of Cyprus, where he visited Queen Charlotte of Bourbon, 
2 CONC his relative.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodes
2 CONT From there, he went to Rhodes, unaware that the plague reigned there. He was stricken with it and died there on 12 August 1414 after making his will10, three days before, in a vineyard, near a village in Rhodes. The Knights of St.
2 CONC  John gave him a magnificent funeral in the church of St. John of Rhodes, for which his widow showed her gratitude by privileges which she granted in Laval to the Commander of Thévalle.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following year at Easter, the count's retinue led by Jean Ouvrouin, arrived in Laval, bringing back his steed and his weapons11.
2 CONT
2 CONT Succession
2 CONT As all these children, at the death of their father, were minors, there was a lawsuit for their guardianship between Raoul de Montfort, their ancestor, and Anne, their mother. It was awarded to the latter, by sentence of the justi
2 CONC ce of Le Mans, from which there was an appeal to the parliament, which confirmed this judgment by a decree in 1417.
2 CONT
2 CONT The abbey of Evron, grateful for his beneficence, placed his coat of arms in one of the stained-glass windows of its church with this legend, now erased: Monsieur de Laval, grand augmentation of this monastery12.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes and 
2 CONT Second quarter by inheritance. He was the grandson of Marie de Léon. First half by marriage to Anne de Laval.
2 CONT Gavere is a municipality in East Flanders in the county of Aalst, not to be confused with Le Gâvre, near Nantes.
2 CONT Abbé Angot, "Saint-Gervais et Saint-Protais de Brée, monographie paroissiale.", 1884 [1] [archive]
2 CONT contract was made at Vitré, in the presence of the young couple, their fathers, the duke of Brittany, Adam Chastelain, bishop of Mans, Anselme de Chantemerle, bishop of Rennes, and many lords. It was ratified by letters patent of 
2 CONC the king and a decree of the parliament of 15 January 1405.
2 CONT Chronologie historique des sires, puis comtes de Laval, 1784, t. II, p. 864-875.
2 CONT banner of the banneret was square, while that of the baron was with pennons and tail.
2 CONT Maucourt de Bourjolly mentions the lords of Champchevrier, Montault, Tucé, Jean Chabot, Jean de Gachanges, Jean des Vaux, Jean II de la Chapelle-Rainsouin
2 CONT Maucourt de Bourjolly cites Jean Ouvroin, Gervais Auvé, Guyon Turpin, Jean d'Arquenay, Bertrand de Parthenay, Jean d'Anthenaise, Jean de Montbourchier, Jean de la Boissière, Jean de Karadeuc, Macé de Quatrebarbes, Grongnet de Vass
2 CONC é, Bobin de Vendel, Jean le Cornu, Guillaume d'Aron, Targis de Vassé, Jean de Gevigné, Jean de la Croix, Le Bidal d'Anthenaise, Pierre de Montenault, Jean Neveu, Jean de Quatrebarbes, Maurice de la Pommeraie, Macé de Grasmesnil, P
2 CONC ierre and Jean du Taillis, Jean L'enfant, Pierre and Henri d'Orange, Guillaume de Sacé, Jean de la Feuillée, Fouques de la Bongère, Jean d'Echenets, Jean de Lantepoutre.
2 CONT André Duchesne, History of the House of Montmorency, p. 573.—De la Roque, Maison d'Harcourt, vol. 1, p. 676.
2 CONT will, dated August 9, 1414, is signed by Jean Ouvroin, lord of Poligné, by Bertrand de Parthenay, Allain de la Haye, and Jean de Brouassin.
2 CONT M. Foucault, Les seigneurs de Laval, Laval, p. 199. In it, he gives details of the announcement of the count's death to the people of Laval and his family.
2 CONT Gérault, Notice historique sur Evron.
