2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=65aba089-c39d-4fd4-ad0b-4b916f8f1162&tid=7047470&pid=856169671
1 NOTE John II (of France), in French, Jean II, called The Good (1319-64), king 
2 CONT of France (1350-64), the son of King Phillip VI. During his reign he 
2 CONT debased the coinage to raise funds for the Hundred Years' War. John was 
2 CONT captured in 1356 by Edward, the Black Prince, at the Battle of Poitiers 
2 CONT and was imprisoned in England. His son Charles, later Charles V, acted as 
2 CONT regent during his captivity. John was returned to France in 1360 to raise 
2 CONT his ransom under the terms of the Peace of Brétigny. Because he was 
2 CONT unsuccessful in collecting the 3 million crowns required, in 1364 John 
2 CONT honorably resumed his captivity in England, where he died.
1 NOTE John II the Good (French: Jean II le Bon) (April 16, 1319 – April 8, 1364), was King of France 1350–1364, Duke of Normandy and Count of Anjou and Maine 1332–1350, Count of Poitiers 1344–1350, and Duke of Guienne 1345–1350. John, a
2 CONC  member of the Valois Dynasty, was the son of Philippe VI and Jeanne of Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 28, 1332, at the age of 13, he was married to Bonne of Luxembourg, daughter of John the Blind of Luxemburg, king of Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was crowned King of France in 1350 in the cathedral at Reims. As king, Jean surrounded himself with poor administrators, preferring to enjoy the good life his wealth as king brought. The men he relied on to administer his kingd
2 CONC om were brutal thieves but eventually King Jean changed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Battle of Poitiers in 1356 against Edward, the Black Prince (son of King Edward III of England), Jean suffered a humiliating defeat and was taken as captive back to England. While negotiating a peace accord, he was at first
2 CONC  held in the Savoy Palace, then at Windsor, Hertford, Somerton Castle in Lincolnshire and finally in the Tower of London. As a prisoner of the English, the King of France was granted royal privileges, permitted to travel about, an
2 CONC d to enjoy a regal lifestyle. A local tradition in St Albans is that he was also held in a house in that town, at the site of the 15th century Fleur de Lys inn, before he was taken to Hertford Castle. There is a sign on the inn to
2 CONC  that effect, but apparently no evidence to confirm the tradition [1].
2 CONT
2 CONT The treaty of Brétigny signed in 1360 set his ransom at 3,000,000 crowns. In keeping with the honor between himself and the English King Edward III, and leaving his son Louis of Anjou in English-held Calais as a replacement hostag
2 CONC e, Jean was allowed to return to France to raise his ransom funds.
2 CONT
2 CONT While King Jean tried to raise the money, his son Louis, accorded the same royal dignity, easily escaped from the English. An angry King Jean, believing his son had broken royal honor, and unable to raise his ransom, surrendered h
2 CONC imself again to the English. He arrived in England in early 1364, looked upon by ordinary citizens and English royalty alike with great admiration. Accordingly, he was held as an honored prisoner in the Savoy Palace but died a few
2 CONC  months later.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Jean died in London in 1364 and his body was returned to France, where he was interred in the royal chambers at Saint Denis Basilica.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded by his son, Charles V.
1 NOTE Il effectue de nombreuses dâevaluations pour faire face aux dâepenses de la cour.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1356, il est fait prisonnier par le fils d'Edouard III d'Angleterre, le Prince de Galles Edouard, áa Poitiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Le fils de Jean le Bon, le futur Charles V, assure la râegence pendant la captivitâe de son páere, râeprime la jacquerie et la râevolte d'Etienne Marcel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Le Dauphin signe le traitâe de Brâetigny le 8 mai 1360, abandonnant áa l'Angleterre entre autres l'Aquitaine, et paie la ranðcon de trois millions d'âecus d'or pour la libâeration du roi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Le roi Jean, apprenant qu'un des otages, son autre fils Louis d'Anjou, s'est âechappâe considáere qu'il doit ãetre fidáele áa sa parole et retourne áa Londres, oáu il meurt en 1364.
1 NOTE KNOWN AS "THE GOOD"; "OF VALOIS"; KING OF FRANCE (INTERMITTENTLY) 1350-1364
2 CONT (ACCEDED 8/22/1350, AND CROWNED AT THEIMS 9/26/1350); BEARER OF THE SWAN BADGE
1 NOTE John II (1319-64), known as �the Good� - �the Generous� - became king of France in 1350. Being hard pressed by the king of Navarre and the English, and accused of maladministration of the finances, he surrendered the management of
2 CONC  them to the States-general. Defeated at Poitiers (1356), he was taken prisoner to England, but returned after the treaty of Bretigny (1360). But the Duke of Anjou, whom he left as hostage, having fled, John thought it his duty to
2 CONC  go back to England, where he finished his days. [World Wide Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1935]
