1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Anne de Laval (1385 - 25 January 1466) was a medieval French noblewoman. She was the daughter of Jeanne de Laval-Tinténiac and her second husband Guy XII de Laval (died 1412), governor of Britanny and baron of Lava
2 CONC l (Jeanne's first husband had been Bertrand du Guesclin).
2 CONT
2 CONT Titles
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the hereditary dame of Laval, hereditary baronne of Vitré, hereditary countess of Rennes, of Châtillon, of Gavre, of Acquigny, of Aubigné, Courbeveille, hereditary dame of Tinténiac, of Bécherel and of Romillé. She inherit
2 CONC ed all these titles from the successive accidental deaths of her two brothers, Guy and Francois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 January 1404, as daughter and sole heir of Guy XII de Laval, she married Jean de Montfort, lord of Montfort and of Kergolay, and from them were descended several bishops and the greatest lords of Britanny[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the conditions of the marriage was that any children born to it would bear the name and arms of Laval. (The same condition had already been applied to the marriage of Emma de Laval with Mathieu II de Montmorency.) Neverthel
2 CONC ess, Jean de Montfort renounced the name and title he had been born with to take up the name of Guy XIII de Laval, better to associate himself with his wife Anne's power. Guy and Anne had 5 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy XIV de Laval, 
2 CONT
2 CONT André de Lohéac, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis de Laval, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne de Laval (Dame de Campzillon), who in 1424 married Louis I de Bourbon-Vendôme, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine de Laval (Dame de Chauvigny) who married Guy de Chauvigny vicomte de Brosse 
