0 @N6000000003051134329@ NOTE When already pregnant, she went swimming in the ocean and was seduced and/or raped by an unidentified creature from beyond the sea: "bestea Neptuni Quinotauri similis". Such fantastic legends are quite common, not only in the anci
1 CONC ent world, but in European tradition as well. Usually they are symbolic or allegorical, masking concrete historical fact behind their fabulous facade. In this case, it may well indicate a pedigree transmitted through her, as in Ju
1 CONC daism. (See Boaz Anfortas)
0 @N6000000003051134338@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Basina, Queen of Thuringia", retrieved 4 Oct 07]
1 CONT Basina was queen of Thuringia in the middle of the fifth century. She left her husband king Bisinus and went to Roman Gaul. She herself took the initiative to ask for the hand of Childeric I, king of the Franks, and married him. F
1 CONC or as she herself said, "I want to have the most powerful man in the world, even if I have to cross the ocean for him". This remark of her may have been related to Childeric's successful invasion of the Roman Empire and his attemp
1 CONC t to settle a Frankish kingdom on Roman soil.
1 CONT
1 CONT Basina's name is probably Low Franconian for 'female boss'. She is the mother of the man who is remembered as the founder of the Frankish realm and modern France. She (not her husband Childeric) named her son Chlodovech, but he is
1 CONC  better remembered under his Latinized name Clovis I. The simple fact that Chlodovech's name comes from Basina is remarkable since it was a common practice for the Franks to name a son after a member of the family of the male-line
1 CONC  of ancestors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Through the ages historians have been intrigued by the story of Basina since she obviously acted as a player and not as bystander ? which is not uncommon for the women of the Franks, but highly uncommon for the Romans.
