0 @N6000000003051240653@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Ingund, wife of Clotaire I", retrieved 4 Oct 07]
1 CONT Ingonde, Ingund, or Ingunda (born c.499, Thuringia) was the daughter of King Baderic of Thuringia (c.480 - c.529. She was the wife of Clotaire I and queen of the Franks. She was the mother of Charibert I, Guntram, and Sigebert I. 
1 CONC She was the sister of Clotaire's wife Aregund.
0 @N6000000003319677936@ NOTE
1 CONT wft 5:1563
0 @N6000000003495155404@ NOTE
1 CONT Sister of previous wife Ingund
1 CONT
1 CONT Arégonde, Aregund, or Aregunda (French: Arnegonde de Worms) (Worms, c.515-573) was the wife of Clotaire I, king of the Franks, and the mother of Chilperic I of Neustria. She was the sister of Ingund, one of Clotaire's other wives.
1 CONC  She may have been born sometime between 500 and 510 and may have died sometime between 580 and 590.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her sepulchre was discovered in 1959 in the Saint Denis Basilica by archaeologist Michel Fleury. It contained remarkably well-preserved clothing items and jewellery.
1 CONT
1 CONT In an episode of the television series "Digging for the Truth" (aired in May 2006), host Josh Bernstein arranged a DNA test of a sample of her remains, to see if it showed any Middle Eastern characteristics. It did not.
