0 @N6000000002240782335@ NOTE He was the eldest son of Clovis I, but was born of an unknown woman, unlike
1 CONT the other four sons of Clovis. An able soldier, he played an important part
1 CONT in his father's campaigns against the Visigoths. His quarter of his father's
1 CONT lands were those in the north (future Austrasia), those most frequently
1 CONT attacked by outsiders. In the 520s Theodoric I gained a share of his brother
1 CONT Clodomir's kingdom when 2 of the dead kings sons were murdered by Theodoric's
1 CONT two other half-brothers, Childebert I and Chlotar I.  Chodovald, escaped.
0 @N6000000003637326689@ NOTE Theodorico I Rey de los Ostrogodos, también conocido como Teodorico Amal, Teodoricus Dietrich El Grande, y conquistador y Rey de Italia, nacido circa 455 y fallecido circa 526. Casó con Teodora y con Audofledis de los Francos Sali
1 CONC ens. Tuvieron a Teodora de los Ostrogodos, Arevagni, Theodegonda de Verona, Amfleda La Mayor, Teodorico II de los Ostrogodos. Envió una embajada a Lodoin rey de los Francos pidiendo a su hija Audefleda en matrimonio, lo cual acept
1 CONC ó Lodoin. He was was, at age seven, offered to the Romans as a hostage of peace in 461. "From the Goths the Romans received as a hostage of peace Theodoric, the young child of Thiudimer, whom we have mentioned above. He had now at
1 CONC tained the age of seven years and was entering upon his eighth. While his father hesitated about giving him up, his uncle Valamir besought him to do it, hoping that peace between the Romans and the Goths might thus be assured. The
1 CONC refore Theodoric was given as a hostage by the Goths and brought to the city of Constantinople to the Emperor Leo and, being a goodly child, deservedly gained the imperial favor."1 He was lived a comfortable life in the court of E
1 CONC mperor Zeno of Byzantium between 461 and 475. However, he had heard that his people, the Ostrogoth's were facing pressures from the Huns and other tribes, and he asked that Zeno give him permission to return to his people and lead
1 CONC  them to new territory. "Although the Emperor was grieved that he should go, yet when he heard this he granted what Theodoric asked, for he was unwilling to cause him sorrow. He sent him forth enriched by great gifts and commended
1 CONC  to his charge the Senate and the Roman People." Tuvo a Moesia por amante con la que tuvo a Ostrogotha Areagni y a Thiudigotho, con Audofledis de los Francos tuvo a Amalasuintha y con Theodora tuvo a Teodora.
1 CONT Fue regente por su nieto Amalarico entre 510 y 526. Llamados los West Goths.
1 CONT Teodorico. Teodorico era miembro de la estirpe de los Amalos como hijo de Teodomiro. En el año 462 fue capturado por los romanos y llevado a Constantinopla como prisionero donde se educó en los valores romanos, conociendo también 
1 CONC las debilidades de la corte en un momento de crisis. Regresó a las tierras ostrogodas donde se convirtió en un potente líder, apoyando a Zenón como emperador de Constantinopla. Sin embargo, no recibió el apoyo imperial para su pro
1 CONC yecto más importante: liberar las tierras italianas controladas por Odoacro. A pesar de carecer del permiso imperial, Teodorico se dirigió hacia la península Itálica para poner en marcha un plan de conquista que fue lento debido a
1 CONC  la resistencia de Odoacro, quien se refugió en Ravena. Teodorico se dirigió a la ciudad y en el año 493 acababa controlándola y asesinando a Odoacro. De esa manera Teodorico y los ostrogodos se hacía dueños de Italia aunque apena
1 CONC s se produjeron cambios en las instituciones existentes, rodeándose de consejeros de origen romano e incluso permitiendo la coexistencia de la Iglesia romana, a pesar de profesar el arrianismo. Intentó armonizar las relaciones ent
1 CONC re romanos y bárbaros, sin renunciar a las diferencias existentes entre ambas sociedades, tarea difícil que pudo sacar adelante. Su política exterior también es digna de mención ya que puso en marcha un amplio plan de alianzas -qu
1 CONC e incluían los enlaces matrimoniales- para convertirse en el líder hegemónico de los pueblos bárbaros. Especialmente intensas fueron las relaciones con los visigodos al convertirse Teodorico en tutor de Amalarico, quien sería el r
1 CONC ey de España. En sus últimos años, Teodorico participó activamente en el conflicto religioso que vivía Oriente.
1 CONT El 30 de Agosto de este año muere Teodorico, el gran monarca de los ostrogodos y rey de Italia. Comienzo de las desavenencias entre los ostrogodos arrianos y los romanos cristianos, que terminan por provocar la decadencia y derrum
1 CONC be del reino ostrogodo en Italia. Su hija Amalasvinta lo sucede
0 @N6000000003949656082@ NOTE According to 6th century Gothic historian Jordanes, Goths originated in Scandinavia.  In 4th cent, B.C.E. they were living along the shores of the Black Sea, and by the end of the 2nd cent C.E. they had migrated to the Danube.  Ab
1 CONC out 375 C.E. the Huns destroyed the Gothic settlements along the Black Sea  and many of the Goths fled to territories controlled by the Romans.   In 378 C.E. Goths killed the Roman Emperor Valens in a battle near the city of Adria
1 CONC nople, Turkey.  At about 400 C.E. Alaric united the Goths in a group called the Visigoths.  They marched west and established kingdoms in Spain and Gaul.  In 507 the Franks conquered the Goths in Gaul.   Visigoths rule in Spain la
1 CONC sted until 711.  
1 CONT After the death of Attila, the leader of the Huns, in 453, the eastern Goths united and were called the Ostragoths.    They settled in the area south of Vienna, Austria and coexisted with the Roman Byzantine empire.
1 CONT Theodoric was sent to Constantinople where he received a Roman education and became a favorite at court.  He became king of the Ostragoths in 471 and decided to carve out a kingdom in Italy which was then ruled by the barbarian, O
1 CONC doacer.  He led about 100,000 (including 75,000 non-combatants) into Italy and fought Odoacer's forces from 488 to 493.  He convinced Odoacer to accept joint rule and then murdered him at the celebratory banquet.  Thus he became s
1 CONC ole ruler with his capitol at Ravenna.  His 33 year reign was characterized by peace, prosperity and tolerance.  He maintained most of the old Roman laws and appointed Romans to civil offices.  He recognized the authority of the E
1 CONC mperor in Constantinople.  He left an architectural legacy, constructing public buildings and repairing roads.  Several of his structures remain standing in Ravenna today, including the church of Sant' Apollinare Nuova, an Arian b
1 CONC aptistry, and his mausoleum.    
1 CONT Built to withstand eternity, his mausoleum resembles no other building in the Roman-Byzantine world.
1 CONT The tomb's domed roof is 36 feet in diameter, weighs about 300 tons and is carved from a single block of marble that was carved on the Istria peninsula in modern Croatia.   They do not know how the roof was cut, transported and ho
1 CONC isted into place.  The purpose was apparently to discourage vandalism.  The roof locks the Lower blocks into place so that would-be vandals would bring the structure down on their head if they tampered with it.  To increase the st
1 CONC ony security, Theodoric included another protective feature which was a system of interlocking masonry joints.  The tomb's walls seem to consist of regular ashlars of squared masonry blocks but in fact many of them are not of stan
1 CONC dard size and therefor are not interchangeable.  The ashlars have joints or protrusions that neatly interlock with the adjacent stones.   These irregular, interlocking joints make it extremely difficult, if not impossible to disma
1 CONC ntle the structure.   This fitted stonework makes the building like a Chinese puzzle.  One particular piece must be removed before the subsequent pieces are chosen and unless the correct piece is chosen, the whole structure remain
1 CONC s intact , a solid interlocked mass.  
1 CONT   (Excerpts from  "The Mystery of Theodoric's Tomb Solved" by Harry Rand, Archaeology Odyssey, Nov-Dec 2003, p 47-53, 57-58)  For video view go to www.turismo.ravenna.it/frame_citta.htm
1 CONT Became king of the Visigoths in 511.  After his elevation to power, the Visigothic treasure was taken to Ravenna.
1 CONT For an extensive history of Theodoric - "History of the Goths" by Hedwig Wolfram, 1987, Univ of Calif Press
