0 @N6000000002187923793@ NOTE Theuderic I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Theuderic I (or Theuderich, Theoderic, or Theodoric; in French, Thierry) (born 484, died 533 or 534) was the Merovingian king of Metz, Rheims, or Austrasia—as it is variously called—from 511 to 533 or 534.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Clovis I, sole king of the Franks, and he inherited Metz in 511 at his father's death. Though, in accord with Salian tradition, the kingdom was divided between Clovis' four surviving sons: Childebert I in Paris, 
1 CONC Chlodomer in Orléans, and Clotaire I in Soissons. Early in his reign, he sent his son Theudebert to battle the Danish King Chlochilaich (a.k.a. Hygelac of Beowulf) who had invaded his realm. Theudebert defeated and killed Chlochil
1 CONC aich.
1 CONT
1 CONT Theuderic got involved in the war between the Thuringian King Hermanfrid and Hermanfrid's only living brother: Baderic. In exchange for his help, Theuderic would receive half of the kingdom. Baderic was defeated, but the land prom
1 CONC ised Theuderic was not given up.
1 CONT
1 CONT The four sons of Clovis then all fought Sigismund of Burgundy and Godomar, kings of the Burgundians. Godomar fled and Sigismund was taken prisoner by Chlodomer. Theuderic married Sigismund's daughter. Godomar rallied the Burgundia
1 CONC n army and won back his kingdom. Chlodomer, aided by Theuderic, defeated Godomar, but died in the fighting at Vézeronce.
1 CONT
1 CONT Theuderic then, with his brother Clotaire and his son, attacked Thuringia to revenge himself on Hermanfrid. Thuringia was conquered, and Clotaire received Radegund, daughter of King Berthar (Hermanfrid's late brother). After makin
1 CONC g a treaty with his brother Childebert, Theuderic died in 534. Upon his death the throne of Metz, though seemingly up for grabs, passed unhindered to Theudebert.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Franks (main history of Frankish kingdoms)
1 CONT     * List of Frankish Kings
1 CONT     * Merovingians
1 CONT     * History of France
0 @N6000000002176649681@ NOTE He was the eldest son of Clovis I, but was born of an unknown woman, unlike
1 CONT the other four sons of Clovis. An able soldier, he played an important part
1 CONT in his father's campaigns against the Visigoths. His quarter of his father's
1 CONT lands were those in the north (future Austrasia), those most frequently
1 CONT attacked by outsiders. In the 520s Theodoric I gained a share of his brother
1 CONT Clodomir's kingdom when 2 of the dead kings sons were murdered by Theodoric's
1 CONT two other half-brothers, Childebert I and Chlotar I.  Chodovald, escaped.
0 @N6000000003949902917@ NOTE     King of Metz 511-534
1 CONT     Son of Clovis I, King of the Franks, he inherited Metz in 511 at his death. His son, Theudebert, was already of age when he ascended to the throne. Early in his reign he sent his son to battle King Chlochilaich, a Dane who had
1 CONC  invaded Metz. Theuderic then aided the Thuringian King Hermanfrid against his only living brother Baderic. Baderic was defeated, but the land promised Theuderic was not given. The four sons of Clovis then all fought Sigismund and
1 CONC  Godomar, father and son Kings of the Burgundians. Sigismund was taken prisoner, and Theuderic married his daughter. Godomar was defeated, but Chlodomer, King of Orleans, was also killed in the battle.
1 CONT     Theuderic then, with his brother Chlotar I and son Theudebert, attacked Thuringia to get his revenge with Hermanfrid. Thuringia was conquered, and Chlotar got Radegund, daughter of King Berthar (Harmanfrid's late brother). Aft
1 CONC er making a treaty with his brother Childebert I, King of Paris, Theuderic died in 534. Upon his death the throne of Metz was up for grabs between Chlotar, Childebert, and Theudebert.
