0 @N6000000002187923851@ NOTE Theudebald
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Theudebald or Theodebald (in modern English, Theobald; in French, Thibaud or Théodebald; in German, Theudowald) (c.535–d.555), son of Theudebert I and Deuteria, was the king of Metz, Rheims, or Austrasia—as it's variously called—f
1 CONC rom 547 or 548 to 555.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was only thirteen years of age when he succeeded and of ill health. However, the loyalty of the nobility to his father's memory preserved the peace during his minority. He married Waldrada, daughter of the Lombard king Wacho an
1 CONC d his step-sister (a daughter of his father's second wife). This marriage fortified the alliance betweent Austrasia and Lombardy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nevertheless, Theudebald could not hold on to the conquests of his father in the north of Italia. The Byzantine Emperor Justinian I sent an army under the command of Narses in 552 and, like his father before him, Theudebald avoide
1 CONC d direct confrontation with it.
1 CONT
1 CONT After a prolonged sickness and prostration, he died in 555, without descendants. His realm passed finally outside of the family of Theuderic I and was united to the kingdoms of his great-uncle Clotaire I, who would soon become kin
1 CONC g of all the Franks.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Franks (main history of Frankish kingdoms)
1 CONT     * List of Frankish Kings
1 CONT     * Merovingians
1 CONT     * History of France
