0 @N6000000001353845493@ NOTE On the assassination of his father in 584 he was still in his cradle.He was, however, recognized as king thanks to the devotion of his mother Fredegond and the protection of his uncle Gontran, king of Burgundy.
1 CONT It was not until after the death of his cousin Childebert II in 595 that Clotaire took any active part in affairs. He then endeavoured to enlarge his estates at the expense of Childebert's sons, Theodebert, king of Austrasia, and 
1 CONC Theuderich II, king of Burgundy; but after gaining a victory at Laffaux (597), he was defeated at Dormelles (600) and lost part of his kingdom. After the war between Theodebert and Theuderich and their death, the nobles of Austras
1 CONC ia and Burgundy appealed to Clotaire who, after putting Brunhilda to death, became master of the whole of the Frankish kingdom (613). He was obliged, however, to make great concessions to the aristocracy to whom he owed his victor
1 CONC y. By the constitution of Oct. 18, 614, he gave legal force to canons which had been voted some days previously by a council convened at Paris, butnot without attempting to modify them by numerous restrictions. He extended the com
1 CONC petence of the ecclesiastical tribunals, suppressed unjust taxes and undertook to select the counts from the districts they had to administer. Clotaire did not unify the administration, however; he maintained separate mayors of th
1 CONC e palace for the three districts over which he ruled. In 623 he made his son Dagobert king of the Austrasians, and gradually subdued all the provinces that had formerly belonged to Childebert II. He also guaranteed a certain measu
1 CONC re of independence to the nobles of Burgundy, giving them the option of having a special mayor of the palace or of dispensing with that officer. These concessions procured him a reign of comparative tranquility.
1 CONT He died on Oct. 18, 629, and was buried at Paris in the church of St.Vincent, afterward known as St. Germain des Prés
