0 @N6000000002188311823@ NOTE Theudebert II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Theudebert II (French: Thibert or Théodebert; 586 – 612), King of Austrasia (595 – 612), was the son and heir of Childebert II. He received the kingdom of Austrasia plus the cities (civitates) of Poitiers, Tours, Vellay, Bordeaux,
1 CONC  and Châteaudun, as well as the Champagne, the Auvergne, and Transjurane Alemannia, on the death of his father in 595, but was dominated by his grandmother Brunhilda, whom he succeeded in driving away in 599.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 599, he and his brother Theuderic II were at war. Theuderic defeated him at Sens, but then allied against their cousin Clotaire II and defeated him at Dormelles (near Montereau), thereby laying their hands on a great portion of
1 CONC  Neustria (600–604). At this point, however, the two brothers took up arms against each other; Theuderic defeated him at Étampes and he refused to aid his brother when Theuderic's kingdom was invaded by Clotaire in 605. In 610, he
1 CONC  extorted Alsace from his brother and Theuderic took up arms against him, yet again. Theudebert II was defeated handily at Toul and at Tolbiac in 612. He was locked up in a monastery at the order of his grandmother Brunhilda, and 
1 CONC assassinated with his son Merovech.
1 CONT
1 CONT His daughter, Emma, married King Eadbald of Kent and had a son, Eorcenberht, who was also a king of Kent.
1 CONT Theudebert II
1 CONT Merovingian Dynasty
1 CONT Born: 586 Died: 612
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Childebert II King of Austrasia
1 CONT 595–612 Succeeded by
1 CONT Theuderic II
0 @N6000000002240782128@ NOTE Younger son of the Merovingian Childebert II, he succeeded his father as king
1 CONT of Burgundy in 595. Cooperation with his brother, king Theodebert II of
1 CONT Austrasia II, was led to discord, and in 612 Theodoric, supported by his
1 CONT grandmother, Brunhild, overthrew his brother. His death shortly after led,
1 CONT after the weeks-long reign of his young son, Sigebert II, to the
1 CONT reunification of the Frankish lands under Chlotar II.
