1 CONT Æthelbert was King of the Jutish kingdom of Kent and the earliest English royal convert to Christianity. According to Bede, King Æthelbert was the third English ruler after ¥lle of Sussex and Ceawlin of Wessex to exercise Imperium
1 CONC , overlordship, over all the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms south of the river Humber. The stages by which he achieved this position and the period during which he exercised it are alike obscure. Bede also tells us that ¥thelbert reigned fo
1 CONC r the incredibly long period of fifty-six years (560-616). This may be one of Bede's very rare chronological mistakes: it is possible that ¥thelbert did not become King of Kent until as late as c. 590.
1 CONT
1 CONT ¥thelbert married a Frankish princess named Bertha, daughter of King Charibert (d. 567), himself a grandson of Clovis (d. 511), the first Christian King of the Franks. When the marriage took place (perhaps c. 580), Bertha was acco
1 CONC mpanied to Kent by a Christian entourage which included a bishop named Liudhard, and she had a Roman building just outside Canterbury restored for her use as a church. So, as Bede says, 'some knowledge of the Christian faith had a
1 CONC lready reached' King ¥thelbert before the arrival of missionaries sent by Pope Gregory I. There may have been more to it than this. ¥thelbert's marriage may have indicated a degree of subjection to Frankish overlordship. Bertha an
1 CONC d Liudhard may have been pressing for the King's conversion to his overlord's faith. ¥thelbert may have given some sort of commitment to Christianity, even if it did not go as far as conversion and baptism, before the arrival of t
1 CONC he Roman missionaries. Indeed, knowledge of the King's interest may have been among the factors impelling Pope Gregory to despatch the mission at the time he did.
1 CONT
1 CONT These are speculative matters, but it is at least worth pondering the proposition that the background to ¥thelbert's conversion may have been a little more complicated than Bede's informants at Canterbury let on (or even realised)
1 CONC . What we can be sure about is that the dynastic connections between the Jutish and Frankish royal houses formed only one strand in a network of contacts that bound sixth-century Kent closely to her neighbours across the Channel. 
1 CONC There may have been Frankish settlers in Kent and Jutish communities in the Rhineland under Frankish rule. Artefacts of Frankish type (e.g. weapons, glassware, jewellery) have been found in Kentish graves, jewellery of Kentish man
1 CONC ufacture in Frankish ones. Exotic objects were reaching Kent from the distant Mediterranean, and among the commodities exchanged for them were almost certainly slaves such as Pope Gregory saw in the Roman slave-market.
1 CONT
1 CONT Late sixth-century Kent was rich. It may be that this wealth had had far-reaching effects upon the social structure, and through the
1 CONT dislocation involved upon the hold of traditional paganism upon people's minds and hearts: so at least anthropologists like to explain barbarian conversion. Certainly the King and his court would have known of a world of richer, m
1 CONC ore powerful and imposing rulers elsewhere; and would have known that all of them were protected and upheld by the Christian God.
1 CONT
1 CONT Augustine arrived at Thanet, then an island, in eastern Kent in 597. After a preliminary meeting with him there, ¥thelbert granted him permission to proceed to the seat of the royal court at Canterbury. It was thus that Canterbury
1 CONC  became, as it has remained, the chief seat of the Christian church in England. ¥thelbert himself was converted and baptised - in view of speculations above, perhaps we should say definitively converted - at a date unknown. The li
1 CONC ttle that we know of his actions as a Christian King suggests that he took his new role very seriously. He gave Augustine sites in Canterbury for the foundation of his cathedral church and outside the town for the establishment of
1 CONC  a monastery (later known as St. Augustine's). He endowed the new church with additional properties, probably having the grants recorded in written form. He assisted in the foundation of bishoprics at Rochester and London. He stro
1 CONC ngly encouraged the conversion of his subjects. He issued a code of laws for his people, whose opening clause makes it unambiguously plain that the new church was to enjoy royal protection. He helped Augustine in difficult negotia
1 CONC tions with the British clergy of Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Though temporary setbacks were to occur after ¥thelbert's death in 616, the King had taken positive steps to ensure the establishment of the Christian church in Anglo-Saxon England. Very far-reaching consequences for the developme
1 CONC nt of English civilisation were to flow from this commitment. In a letter to ¥thelbert in 601 Pope Gregory welcomed him into the community of Christian rulers. ¥thelbert was another Constantine; his name would live forever: how ri
1 CONC ght the Pope was. What ¥thelbert thought about it all we do not know. He could rejoice in the protection of a God whom he was encouraged to believe more powerful than his ancestral gods. He had been introduced to techniques of rul
1 CONC ership, notably the use of writing, which might dignify and enhance his authority. He was the friend and patron of clergy who commanded potent rituals. Christianity could make a king more imposing. Its coming was to transform Angl
1 CONC o-Saxon kingship in the course of the seventh century.
1 CONT
1 CONT [From "The British Monarchy", www.royal.gov.uk]
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the later chronicler Bede, the most famous of these kings was Ethelberht, king of Kent (reigned c.560-616), who married Bertha, the Christian daughter of the king of Paris, and who became the first English king to be 
1 CONC converted to Christianity (St Augustine's mission from the Pope to Britain in 597 during Ethelberht's reign prompted thousands of such conversions). Ethelberht was a lawgiver as well as a warrior-king - his law code was the first 
1 CONC to be written in any Germanic language and included 90 laws. Ethelberht's influence extended both north and south of the river Humber: Ethelberht's nephew became king of the East Saxons, and his daughter married king Edwin of Nort
1 CONC humbria (died 633) who 'ruled with greater power over all the peoples who inhabit Britain, English and Britons as well, except only the people of Kent' (Bede).
1 CONT
1 CONT [Wikipedia, "Aethelbert of Kent", retrieved 25 Dec 07]
1 CONT Æthelberht (also Æthelbert, Aethelberht, Aethelbert, or Ethelbert) (c. 560 - February 24, 616) was King of Kent from about 580 or 590 until his death. In his Ecclesiastical History of the English People, the monk Bede lists Aethel
1 CONC berht as the third king to hold imperium over other Anglo-Saxon kingdoms. In the late 9th century Anglo-Saxon Chronicle Æthelberht is referred to as a bretwalda, or "Britain-ruler". He was the first English king to convert to Chri
1 CONC stianity.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Eormenric, whom he succeeded as king, according to the Chronicle. He married Bertha, daughter of Charibert, king of the Franks, thus building an alliance with the most powerful state in Western Europe at that tim
1 CONC e; the marriage probably took place before Æthelberht came to the throne. The influence of Bertha may have led to Pope Gregory I?s decision to send Augustine as a missionary from Rome. Augustine landed on the Isle of Thanet in eas
1 CONC t Kent in 597. Shortly thereafter, Æthelberht was converted to Christianity, churches were established and wider-scale conversion to Christianity began. Æthelberht provided the new church with land in Canterbury, at what came to b
1 CONC e known as St Augustine's Abbey, thus establishing one of the foundation-stones of what ultimately became the Anglican church.
1 CONT
1 CONT Æthelberht?s code of laws for Kent, the earliest written code in any Germanic language, instituted a complex system of fines. Kent was rich, with strong trade ties to the continent, and it may be that Æthelberht instituted royal c
1 CONC ontrol of trade. Coinage began circulating in Kent during his reign for the first time since the Anglo-Saxon invasion.
1 CONT
1 CONT Æthelberht was later canonised for his role in establishing Christianity among the Anglo-Saxons. His feast day was originally February 24, but was changed to February 25.
1 CONT
1 CONT Death and succession
1 CONT Æthelberht died on February 24th, 616, and was succeeded by his son, Eadbald, who was not a Christian?Bede says he had been converted but went back to his pagan faith, though he ultimately did become a Christian king. Eadbald outr
1 CONC aged the church by marrying his stepmother, which was contrary to Church law, and by refusing to accept baptism. Sæberht of the East Saxons also died at about this time, and was succeeded by three sons, none of whom were Christian
1 CONC ; the subsequent revolt against Christianity, and the expulsion of Mellitus, their bishop, may have been a reaction to Kentish overlordship after Æthelberht?s death as much as a pagan opposition to Christianity.
1 CONT
1 CONT In addition to Eadbald, it is possible that Æthelberht had another son, Æthelwald. The evidence for this is a papal letter to Justus, archbishop of Canterbury from 619 to 625, in which a king named Aduluald is referred to, and who
1 CONC  is apparently different from Audubald, which refers to Eadbald. There is no agreement among modern scholars on how to interpret this: "Aduluald" might be intended as a representation of "Æthelwald", and hence this may be an indic
1 CONC ation of another king, perhaps a subking of west Kent; or it may be merely a scribal error which should be read as referring to Eadbald.
1 CONT
1 CONT Æthelberht was later canonised for his role in establishing Christianity among the Anglo-Saxons. His feast day was originally February 24, but was changed to February 25.
