0 @N6000000002187923870@ NOTE Alaric II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Alaric II, also known as Alarik, Alarich, and Alarico in Spanish or Alaricus in Latin (d. 507) succeeded his father Euric in 485 as king of the Visigoths. His dominions included not only the whole of Hispania except its north-west
1 CONC ern corner but also Aquitaine and the greater part of an as-yet undivided Gallia Narbonensis.
1 CONT
1 CONT In religion Alaric was an Arian, like all the early Visigothic nobles, but he greatly mitigated the persecuting policy of his father Euric toward the Catholics and authorized them to hold in 506 the council of Agde. He was on unea
1 CONC sy terms with the Catholic bishops of Arles as epitomized in the career of the Frankish Caesarius, bishop of Arles, born at Châlons and appointed bishop in 503. Caesarius was suspected of conspiring with the Burgundians to turn ov
1 CONC er the Arelate to Burgundy, whose king had married the sister of Clovis, so Alaric exiled him for a year safely at Bordeaux in Aquitaine before allowing him to return unharmed when the crisis had passed (Wace, Dictionary).
1 CONT
1 CONT He displayed similar wisdom and liberality in political affairs by appointing a commission to prepare an abstract of the Roman laws and imperial decrees, which should form the authoritative code for his Roman subjects. This is gen
1 CONC erally known as the Breviarium Alaricianum or Breviary of Alaric.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alaric was of a peaceful disposition and endeavoured strictly to maintain the treaty which his father had concluded with the Franks, whose king Clovis I, however, desiring to obtain the Gothic province in Gaul, found a pretext for
1 CONC  war in the Arianism of Alaric. The intervention of Theodoric, king of the Ostrogoths and father-in-law of Alaric, proved unavailing. The two armies met in 507 at the Battle of Vouillé, near Poitiers, where the Goths were defeated
1 CONC  and their king, who took to flight, was overtaken and slain, it is said, by Clovis himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT His legitimate son Amalaric was still a child, so he was succeeded by his illegitimate son, Gesalec.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT     * Edward Gibbon, History of the Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire Chapter 38
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Euric King of the Visigoths
1 CONT 485–507 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Gesalec
0 @N6000000003949665947@ NOTE Alaric II, also known as Alarik, Alarich, and Alarico in Spanish and Portuguese or Alaricus in Latin (d. 507) succeeded his father Euric in 485 and became eighth king of the Visigoths. His dominions included not only the whole of 
1 CONC Hispania except its north-western corner but also Gallia Aquitania and the greater part of an as-yet undivided Gallia Narbonensis.
1 CONT In 486 Alaric II denied refuge to Afranius Syagrius, the former ruler of the Domain of Soissons who was defeated by Clovis I. Alarmed by a summons from Clovis, Alaric imprisoned and repatriated Syagrius back to Clovis I, where he 
1 CONC was decapitated.
1 CONT In 506, the Visigoths captured the city of Dertosa in the Ebro valley. There they captured the Roman usurper Peter and had him executed.
1 CONT In religion Alaric was an Arian, like all the early Visigothic nobles, but he greatly mitigated the persecuting policy of his father Euric toward the Catholics and authorized them to hold in 506 the council of Agde. He was on unea
1 CONC sy terms with the Catholic bishops of Arles as epitomized in the career of the Frankish Caesarius, bishop of Arles, born at Châlons and appointed bishop in 503. Caesarius was suspected of conspiring with the Burgundians to turn ov
1 CONC er the Arelate to Burgundy, whose king had married the sister of Clovis, so Alaric exiled him for a year safely at Bordeaux in Aquitania before allowing him to return unharmed when the crisis had passed (Wace, Dictionary).
1 CONT He displayed similar wisdom and liberality in political affairs by appointing a commission to prepare an abstract of the Roman laws and imperial decrees, which should form the authoritative code for his Roman subjects. This is gen
1 CONC erally known as the Breviarium Alaricianum or Breviary of Alaric.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Battle of Vouillé and aftermath
1 CONT
1 CONT Alaric endeavoured strictly to maintain the treaty which his father had concluded with the Franks. The Frankish king Clovis I, however, desired to obtain the Gothic province in Gaul and he found a pretext for war in the Arianism o
1 CONC f Alaric. The intervention of Theodoric, king of the Ostrogoths and father-in-law of Alaric, proved unavailing. The two armies met in 507 at the Battle of Vouillé, near Poitiers, where the Goths were defeated and their king, who t
1 CONC ook to flight, was overtaken and slain, it is said, by Clovis himself. As a consequence of their defeat the Visigoths lost all their possessions in Gaul to the Franks, except Septimania (i.e. the western region of Gallia Narbonens
1 CONC is, which includes the contemporary Arles and the Provence). Alaric was succeeded by his illegitimate son, Gesalec, because his legitimate son Amalaric was still a child.
0 @N6000000002187923536@ NOTE Waltari (Lombard king)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Waltari son of Wacho from his third wife Silinga, was a king of the Lombards from ca. 532. He was an infant king, and the rule was administered by Audoin. Audoin probabaly killed Waltari before he reached manhood, in order to gain
1 CONC  the throne for himself in ca. 539, and led the Lombards into Pannonia.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Wacho King of the Lombards Followed by:
1 CONT Audoin
