0 @N6000000000337406704@ NOTE Weis:  "the Parricide" - King of Cologne, murdered 509 by agents of Clovis I.
0 @N6000000000437070633@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> I, Of The Franks "The Great" "The Riparian" "Clovis I was also the most powerful Merovingian king. He unified theFranks under his rule. By the time he died in 511, he had conquered northe
1 CONC rn and southwestern Gaul and additional land in what is now western Germany. The kingdom was then divided among four of Clovis' sons, who added southeastern Gauland other territory to the realm. However, a process of further divid
1 CONC ing the kingdom led to numerous civil wars that severely weakened the dynasty. In 751, the last Merovingian king, Childeric III, was replaced by Pepin the Short, the first ruler from the Carolingian dynasty (see Pepin the Short). 
1 CONC "
1 CONT
1 CONT ------------- "Clovis inherited his father's kingdom in 481, at which time he unified theSalian and Ripurian Franks. In 486 he defeated the Roman general Syagrius who ruled northern Gaul out of Soissons. By 493 he married the Burg
1 CONC undian princess Clotilda. In 496, after defeating the Alamanni, he was baptized, thus becoming the first Christian ruler of post-Roman Gaul. By 506 the Alamanni were subdued,and the next year Clovis finished his expansion by takin
1 CONC g Aquitaine from the weak Visigothic king Alaric II. On Clovis' death in 511, the kingdom was split between Chlodomer (Orleans), Childebert (Paris), Chlotar (Soissons), and Theuderic (Metz). " -------------------------------------
1 CONC ---- 1  NAME Clovis I "The Great" King of the /Franks/ 1  NAME Clovis I "The Great"/Meroving/ 1  BIRT 2DATE 463 1  BIRT 2  DATE ABT. 466 2  PLAC Rheims,Marne, Loire-Atlantique,France 1  DEAT 2  DATE 511 1  DEAT 2  DATE 27 NOV 511 
1 CONC 2  PLAC Saint Pierre, France
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [De La Pole.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources: RC 303; AF; Kraentzler 1636, 1777, 1780; "The Franks" by Edward James; Collins; Pfafman; Women in the Wall. Franks: United all the Franks. It was not done peacefully. Clovis' methods included conquest and murder. He perso
1 CONC nally killed one Frankish king and his brotherafter they had been turned over to him. See Chapter3 of "The Franks." When hedied in 511, the kingdom was divided among his four sons. In the next 50 years, the Franks and their Merovi
1 CONC ngian dynasty extended the power and influence ofmuch of western Europe and became the dominant Germanic successor kingdom tothe Roman Empire. Became a Christian and converted the Franks to Christianity. RC: Clovis I "the Great," 
1 CONC King of the Salic Franks, 481-511. King of France. K: Clovis I, King of the Franks. Died 493 (pickedup a marriage date?) Also called Chlodwig. Pfafman: Clovis (Louis) I, the Great. Sole King of Franks 511,King of Salic Franks 481-
1 CONC 511. Became a devout Catholic responsible for Catholicism instead of Arianism in large portion of Western Europe. Great statesman.Conquered Central and Southern France and Gaul, later conquered the RipuerianFranks. His dominions b
1 CONC ecame France.First important ruler of the Merovingian dynasty. He succeeded his father, Childeric I, as king of the Salian Franks at age 15. His career focused largely on forging the Salian Franks on the northernRhine River and th
1 CONC e Ripuarian Franks on the lowerRhine into a single dominion.He began with a victory in 486 over Syagrius, the last Roman governor in northern Gaul. By 493, when he married the Burgundian princess Clotilda (later canonized as St. C
1 CONC lotilda), Clovis had defeated many petty princes whose territorieshad surrounded his capital at Soissons. He next came into conflict in 496 withthe confederation  of Germanic tribes known asthe Alamanni, who inhabited landeast of 
1 CONC his domains. According to legend, it was only by invoking the God of his Christian wife, Clotilda , that he defeatedhis enemy. Clotilda was almost certainly instrumental in Clovis's conversion to Christianity, and he was baptized 
1 CONC in 496. He became the champion of orthodox Christians in every part of Gauland was supported effectively by the church in all his campaigns. He continuedto fight the Alamanni, who were completely conque
0 @N6000000000437402144@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> King Of Cologne
0 @N6000000001353843712@ NOTE Murdered in 509 by agents of his kinsman, Clovis I. [Roderick W. Stuart, Royalty for Commoners, 3rd ed., Genealogical Publishing Company, Baltimore MD, 1998
0 @N6000000001353843943@ NOTE CLOVIS (Chlodovech) (c. 466-511], king of the Salian Franks, son of Childeric I, whom he succeeded in 481. At that date the Salian Franks had advanced as far as the river Somme, and the centre of their power was at Tournai. On thi
1 CONC s history of Clovis between the years 481 and 486the records are silent. In 486 he attacked Syagrius, a Roman general who, after the fall of the western empire in 476, had carved out for himself a principality south of the Somme, 
1 CONC and is called by Gregory of Tours "rex Romanorum." After being defeated by Clovis at the battle ofSoissons, Syagrius sought refuge with the Visigothic king Alaric II, who handed him over to the conqueror. Henceforth Clovis fixed h
1 CONC is residence at Soissons, which was in the midst of public lands, e.g., Berny-Rivière, Juvigny, etc. The episode of the vase of Soissons has a legendary character, and all that it proves is the deference shown by the pagan king to
1 CONC  the orthodox clergy. Clovis undoubtedly extended his dominion over the whole of Belgica Secunda, of which Reims was the capital, and conquered the neighbouring cities. Little is known of the history of these conquests. It appears
1 CONC  that St. Geneviève defended the townof Paris against Clovis for a long period, and that Verdun-sur-Meuse,after a brief stand, accepted an honourable capitulation thanks to St. Euspitius. In 491 some barbarian troops in the servic
1 CONC e of Rome, Arboruchi Thuringians, and even Roman soldiers who could not return to Rome, went over to Clovis and swelled the ranks of his army.
1 CONT In 493 Clovis married a Burgundian princess, Clotilda, niece of Gundobald and Godegesil, joint kings of Burgundy. This princess was a Christian, and earnestly desired the conversion of her husband. Although Clovis allowed his chil
1 CONC dren to be baptized, he remained a pagan himself until the war against the Alamanni, who at the time occupied the country between the Vosges and the Rhine and the neighbourhood of Lake Constance. By pushing their incursions westwa
1 CONC rd they came into collision with Clovis who marched against them and defeated them in the plain of the Rhine. The legend runs that, in the thickest of the fight, Clovis swore that he would be converted to the God of Clotilda if he
1 CONC r God would grant him the victory. After subduing a part of the Alamanni, Clovis went to Reims, where he was baptized by St. Remigius on Christmas day 496, together with 3,000 Franks. The story of the phial of holy oilbrought from
1 CONC  heaven by a white dove for the baptism of Clovis was invented by archbishop Hincmar of Reims three centuries after the event.
1 CONT The baptism of Clovis was an event of very great importance. From that time the orthodox Christians in the kingdom of the Burgundians and Visigoths looked to Clovis to deliver them from their Arian kings. Clovis seems to have fail
1 CONC ed in the case of Burgundy, which was at that time torn by the rivalry between Godegesil and his brother Gundobald. Godegesil appealed for help to Clovis, who defeated Gundobald on the banks of the Ouche near Dijon, and advanced a
1 CONC s far as Avignon (500), but had to retire without being able to retain any of his conquests. Immediately after his departure Gundobald slew Godegesil at Vienne, and seized the whole of the Burgundian kingdom. Clovis was more fortu
1 CONC nate in his war with the Visigoths. Having completed the subjugation of the Alamanni in 506, he marched against the Visigothic king Alaric II in thefollowing year in spite of the efforts of Theodoric, king of the Ostrogoths, to pr
1 CONC event the war. After a decisive victory at Vouillé near Poitiers, in which Clovis slew Alaric with his own hand, the whole of the kingdom of the Visigoths as far as the Pyrenees was added to the Frankish empire, with the exception
1 CONC  of Septimania, which, together with Spain, remained in possession of Alaric's grandson, Amalaric, and Provence, which was seized by Theodoric and annexed to Italy. In 508 Clovis received at Tours the insignia of the consulship fr
1 CONC om the eastern emperor, Anastasius, but the title was purely honorific. The last yearsof his life Clovis spent in Paris, which he made the capital of his kingdom; there he built the church of the Holy Apostles, known later asthe c
1 CONC hurch of Ste. Geneviève. By murdering the petty Frankish kings who reigned at Cambrai, Cologne and other residences he became the soleking of all the Frankish tribes. He died in 511.
1 CONT Clovis was the true founder of the Frankish monarchy. He reigned overthe Salian Franks by hereditary right; over the other Frankish tribesby reason of his kinship with their kings and by the choice of the warriors, who raised him 
1 CONC on the shield, and he governed the Gallo-Romansby right of conquest. He had the Salic law drawn up, doubtless between the years 486 and 507, and seems to have been represented in the cities by a new functionary, the 'graf.' 'comes
1 CONC ,' or count. He owed his success in great measure to his alliance with the church. He took the property of the church under his protection and in 511 convoked a council or Orléans, the canons of which have come down to us. But whi
1 CONC le protecting the church, he maintained his authority over it. He intervened in nominating bishops, and the council of Orléans decided that no one but a priest's son could be ordained clerk without the kings order or the permissio
1 CONC n of the count[blended.FTW]
1 CONT [mergebase.FTW]
1 CONT King of the Salian Franks 481-486
1 CONT King of the Franks 486-511
1 CONT Clovis inherited his father's kingdom in 481, at which time he unified the Salian and Ripurian Franks. In 486 he defeated the Roman general Syagrius who ruled northern Gaul out of Soissons. By 493 he marriedthe Burgundian princess
1 CONC  Clotilda. In 496, after defeating the Alamanni, he was baptized, thus becoming the first Christian ruler of post-Roman Gaul. By 506 the Alamanni were subdued, and the next year Clovis finished his expansion by taking Aquitaine fr
1 CONC om the weak Visigothic king Alaric II. On Clovis' death in 511, the kingdom was split between Chlodomer (Orleans), Childebert (Paris), Chlotar (Soissons), and Theuderic (Metz).
1 CONT [Fix.FTW]
