0 @N6000000002187927378@ NOTE Prefect
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT A prefect (from the Latin praefectus, perfect participle of praeficere: "make in front", i.e., put in charge) is an official of various different types.
1 CONT
1 CONT A prefect's office, department, or area of control is called a prefecture, but in various post-Roman cases there is a prefect without a prefecture or vice versa. The words "prefect" and "prefecture" are also used, more or less con
1 CONC ventionally, to render analogous words in other, especially Romance languages, which may be misleading or even incorrect.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Ancient Rome
1 CONT           o 1.1 Praetorian prefects
1 CONT           o 1.2 Police prefects
1 CONT           o 1.3 Military prefects
1 CONT           o 1.4 Religious prefects
1 CONT     * 2 Feudal times
1 CONT     * 3 Ecclesiastical
1 CONT     * 4 Academic
1 CONT     * 5 Modern sub-national administration
1 CONT     * 6 Police
1 CONT     * 7 See also
1 CONT     * 8 External link
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ancient Rome
1 CONT
1 CONT Praefectus, often with a further qualification, was the formal title of many, fairly low to high-ranking, military or civil officials in the Roman Empire, whose authority was not embodied in their person (as it was with elected Ma
1 CONC gistrates) but conferred by delegation from a higher authority.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Praetorian prefects
1 CONT
1 CONT The Praetorian prefect (Praefectus praetorio) began as the military commander of a general's guard company in the field, then grew in importance as the Praetorian Guard became a potential kingmaker during the Empire. From the Empe
1 CONC ror Diocletian's tetrarchy (c. 300) they became the administrators of the four Praetorian Prefectures, the government level above the (newly created) dioceses and (multiplied) provinces.
1 CONT
1 CONT As Egypt was a special crown domain, a rich and strategic granary, where the Emperor enjoyed an almost pharaonic position unlike any other province or diocese, its head was styled uniquely Praefectus Augustalis, indicating that he
1 CONC  governed in the personal name of the august emperor.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Police prefects
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Praefectus urbi: city prefect, in charge of the administration of Rome.
1 CONT     * Praefectus vigilum: commander of the Vigiles.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Military prefects
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Praefectus alae: commander of a cavalry regiment.
1 CONT     * Praefectus castrorum: camp commandant.
1 CONT     * Praefectus cohortis: commander of a cohort (constituent unit of a legion, or analogous unit).
1 CONT     * Praefectus classis: fleet commander.
1 CONT     * Praefectus equitatus: cavalry commander.
1 CONT     * Praefectus equitum: cavalry commander.
1 CONT     * Praefectus fabrum: officer in charge of engineers and artisans.
1 CONT     * Praefectus legionis: equestrian legionary commander.
1 CONT     * Praefectus legionis agens vice legati: equestrian acting legionary commander.
1 CONT     * Praefectus sociorum: Roman officer appointed to a command function in an ala sociorum (unit recruited among the socii, Italic peoples of a privileged status within the empire).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Religious prefects
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Praefectus urbi: a prefect of the republican era who guarded the city during the annual sacrifice of the feriae latina on Mount Alban in which the Consuls participated. His former title was "custos urbi" ("guardian of the ci
1 CONC ty").
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Feudal times
1 CONT
1 CONT Especially in Middle Latin, "praefectus" was used to refer to various officers – administrative, military, judicial, etc. – usually alongside a more precise term in the vernacular (such as "Burggraf").
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ecclesiastical
1 CONT
1 CONT The term is used by the Roman Catholic Church, which based much of its canon law terminology on Roman law, in several different ways.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * The Roman Curia still has two Prefects, of the Papal Household and the Economic Affairs of the Holy See.
1 CONT     * The title now also attaches to the heads of some Congregations (central departments of the Curia), who are traditionally Cardinals, hence often called "Cardinal-prefect" (if not they are titled "Pro-Prefect").
1 CONT     * A Prefect Apostolic is a cleric (sometimes a Titular Bishop, but normally a priest) in charge of an apostolic prefecture, a type of Roman Catholic territorial jurisdiction fulfilling the fucntions of a diocese, usually in a 
1 CONC missionary area or in a country that is anti-religious, such as the People's Republic of China) but that is not yet given the status of regular diocese. It is usually destined to become one in time.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Academic
1 CONT
1 CONT     * In the context of schools, a prefect is a pupil who has been given limited, trustee-type authority over other pupils in the school, such as a hall monitor or safety patrol.
1 CONT     * In many British and Commonwealth schools (especially but not exclusively public schools), prefects, usually sixth formers, have considerable power and effectively run the school outside the classroom. They were once even all
1 CONC owed to administer corporal punishment (emulating domestic discipline) in some schools (now abolished in the UK and several other countries) under a system of self control, or sometimes used as (generally willing) 'executioner' by
1 CONC  the staff. They usually answer to a senior prefect known as the Head of School (colloquially, Head Boy or Head Girl). However, due to Health and Safety laws the staff have tended to become stricter about what responsibilities pre
1 CONC fects may hold, for fear of being held responsible in case of litigation.
1 CONT     * In United States private residential college preparatory schools; see also "proctor".
1 CONT     * In Sweden, a prefect (prefekt) is the head of a university department.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the United States, formerly in many Catholic high schools this title was given to a member of the faculty ("prefect of discipline" in charge of student attendance, general order and such).
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Modern sub-national administration
1 CONT
1 CONT     * In France (and some former French or Belgian colonies, such as Rwanda), a prefect (préfet) is the State's representative in a région (préfet de région) or département. His agency is called the préfecture, and his circumscrip
1 CONC tion is also called a prefecture in some former French republics. Sub-prefects (sous-préfets, sous-préfecture) operate in the arrondissements under his control.
1 CONT     * In Italy, a prefect (prefetto) is the State's representative in a province (provincia). His agency is called the prefettura.
1 CONT     * In some Spanish-speaking states in Latin America, following a French-type model introduced in Spain itself, prefects were installed as governors; remarkably, in some republics (like Peru) two levels were constructed from the
1 CONC  French model: a prefecture and a department, the one being only part of the other.
1 CONT     * In Romania, a prefect is the governmental representative in a county (judet), in an agency called prefectura.
1 CONT     * In Quebec, a prefect (préfet) is the head of a regional county municipality.
1 CONT     * In Brazil, a prefect (prefeito) is the elected head of the executive branch in a municipality. Larger cities, such as São Paulo, Rio de Janeiro, Curitiba, etc., also have sub-prefects, appointed to their offices by the elect
1 CONC ed prefect.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Police
1 CONT
1 CONT The Prefect of Police (Préfet de police) is the officer in charge of co-ordinating police forces in the various administrative circumscriptions of Paris.
