0 @N6000000002187840066@ NOTE John II of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John II the Good 
1 CONT King of France (more...) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign 22 August 1350 – 8 April 1364 
1 CONT Coronation 26 September 1350, Reims 
1 CONT Titles Count of Anjou and Maine, Duke of Normandy (1332 – 1350)
1 CONT Count of Poitiers (1344 – 1350)
1 CONT Duke of Guyenne (1345 – 1350)
1 CONT John I, Duke of Burgundy (1361-1363)
1 CONT Jure uxoris Count of Auvergne and Boulogne (1349–1360) 
1 CONT Born 16 April 1319(1319-04-16) 
1 CONT Died 8 April 1364 (aged 44) 
1 CONT  Savoy Palace, London, England 
1 CONT Buried Saint Denis Basilica 
1 CONT Predecessor Philip VI 
1 CONT Successor Charles V 
1 CONT Consort Bonne of Bohemia (1315-1349)
1 CONT Joanna I of Auvergne (1326-1360) 
1 CONT Issue Charles V (1338-1380)
1 CONT Louis of Anjou (1339-1384)
1 CONT John, Duke of Berry (1340-1416)
1 CONT Philip II, Duke of Burgundy (1342-1404)
1 CONT Jeanne, Queen of Navarre (1343-1373) 
1 CONT Royal House Valois Dynasty 
1 CONT Father Philip VI (1293-1350) 
1 CONT Mother Joan of Burgundy (1293-1348) 
1 CONT John II (16 April 1319 – 8 April 1364), called the Good (French: Jean le Bon), was Count of Anjou, Count of Maine, and Duke of Normandy from 1332, Count of Poitiers from 1344, Duke of Aquitaine from 1345, and King of France from 1
1 CONC 350 until his death, as well as Duke of Burgundy (as John I) from 1361 to 1363. By his marriage to Joanna I, Countess of Auvergne and Boulogne, he became jure uxoris Count of Auvergne and Boulogne from 1349 to 1360. John was a mem
1 CONC ber of the House of Valois, and was the son of Philippe VI and Jeanne of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT John succeeded his father in 1350 and was crowned at Notre-Dame de Reims. As king, John surrounded himself with poor administrators, preferring to enjoy the good life his wealth as king brought. Later in his reign, he took over mo
1 CONC re of the administration himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1354, John's son-in-law and cousin, Charles II of Navarre, who, in addition to his small Pyrenean kingdom, also held extensive lands in Normandy, was implicated in the assassination of the Constable of France, Charles de la Cer
1 CONC da. Nevertheless, in order to have a strategic ally against the English in Gascony, on 22 February 1354, John signed the Treaty of Mantes with Charles. The peace did not last between the two and Charles eventually struck up an all
1 CONC iance with Henry of Grosmont, the first Duke of Lancaster. The next year (1355), John signed the Treaty of Valognes with Charles, but this second peace lasted hardly longer than the first. In 1355, the Hundred Years' War flared up
1 CONC  again.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Battle of Poitiers (1356) against Edward, the Black Prince, (son of King Edward III of England), John suffered a humiliating defeat and was taken as captive back to England. While negotiating a peace accord, he was at first
1 CONC  held in the Savoy Palace, then at a variety of locations, including Windsor, Hertford, Somerton Castle in Lincolnshire, Berkhamsted Castle in Hertfordshire and briefly at King John's Lodge, formerly known as Shortridges, in East 
1 CONC Sussex. A local tradition in St Albans is that he was held in a house in that town, at the site of the 15th-century Fleur de Lys inn, before he was moved to Hertford. There is a sign on the inn to that effect, but apparently no ev
1 CONC idence to confirm the tradition [1]. Eventually, John was taken to the Tower of London.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a prisoner of the English, John was granted royal privileges, permitting him to travel about and to enjoy a regal lifestyle. At a time when law and order was breaking down in France and the government was having a hard time rai
1 CONC sing money for the defense of the realm, his account books during his captivity show that he was purchasing horses, pets, and clothes while maintaining an astrologer and a court band.[citation needed]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Coin of Jean le Bon (1350-1363).The Treaty of Brétigny (1360) set his ransom at 3,000,000 crowns. Leaving his son Louis of Anjou in English-held Calais as a replacement hostage, John was allowed to return to France to raise the fu
1 CONC nds.
1 CONT
1 CONT While King John tried to raise the money, his son Louis, accorded the same royal dignity, easily escaped from the English. An angry King John surrendered himself again to the English, claiming an inability to pay the ransom as the
1 CONC  reason. The true motive of John's decision remains murky today, with many pointing to the devastation in France caused by war with England and the Jacquerie peasant uprising as likely candidates. His councillors and nearly the wh
1 CONC ole nation was critical of the decision, since they had sacrificed much to raise the ransom. When John arrived in England in early 1364, however, he was viewed with admiration by ordinary citizens and English royalty alike. Althou
1 CONC gh treated with honor while held in the Savoy Palace, he died in London a few months later.
1 CONT
1 CONT His body was returned to France, where he was interred in the royal chambers at Saint Denis Basilica.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Ancestors 
1 CONT 2 Family and children 
1 CONT 3 External links 
1 CONT 4 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Ancestors
1 CONT John's ancestors in three generations
1 CONT
1 CONT         Philip III of France 
1 CONT
1 CONT   Charles of Valois     
1 CONT
1 CONT     Isabella of Aragon 
1 CONT
1 CONT   Philip VI of France     
1 CONT
1 CONT     Charles II of Naples 
1 CONT
1 CONT   Marguerite of Anjou and Maine     
1 CONT
1 CONT     Maria Arpad of Hungary 
1 CONT
1 CONT John II of France     
1 CONT
1 CONT     Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy 
1 CONT
1 CONT   Robert II, Duke of Burgundy     
1 CONT
1 CONT     Yolande of Dreux 
1 CONT
1 CONT   Joan the Lame     
1 CONT
1 CONT     Louis IX of France 
1 CONT
1 CONT   Agnes of France, Duchess of Burgundy     
1 CONT
1 CONT     Marguerite of Provence 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT On July 28, 1332, at the age of 13, John was married to Bonne of Bohemia (d. 1349), daughter of John I (the Blind) of Bohemia. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles V (January 21, 1338–September 16, 1380) 
1 CONT Louis I, Duke of Anjou (July 23, 1339–September 20, 1384) 
